  "lessons": [
    {
      "key": "lesson-13",
      "name": "Pre-Assessment",
      "absolute_position": 1,
      "lockable": true,
      "has_lesson_plan": false,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "assessment": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-13",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-1",
      "name": "What is Data Analysis?",
      "absolute_position": 2,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How can I make use of data?**\n\nIn this lesson, students play *Candidate*—a game that guides them, first-hand, through the process of data analysis. As they compete to run for student government on the most popular policy, students learn to ask strategic questions, collect simple data, and analyze it to make wise choices.",
        "preparation": "- Read the *Candidate* Supplemental Teacher Guide.\n- Print one copy of the *Candidate* Persona Card sheets for every 32 students, then cut the cards apart and shuffle them. It’s okay if you have more cards than students—but every student should have one card.\n- Print one double-sided copy of the *Candidate* Activity Guide for each student.",
        "student_overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How can I make use of data?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll play *Candidate*—a game that guides you, first-hand, through the process of data analysis. As you compete to run for student government on the most popular policy, you'll learn to ask strategic questions, collect simple data, and analyze it to make wise choices.",
        "unplugged": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-1",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-2",
      "name": "Exploring Structured Data",
      "absolute_position": 3,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can information be stored in ways computers can use?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn to work with data represented in rows and columns. They explore tabular datasets in the form of spreadsheets and use datasets to answer questions, pose questions of their own, and identify what kinds of questions can and cannot be answered by data. Students are also introduced to CSV files as a text-based means of representing tabular data.",
        "preparation": "- Prepare blank sheets of paper (one sheet per student).",
        "student_overview": "**How can information be stored in ways computers can use?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll learn to work with data represented in rows and columns. You'll explore tabular datasets in the form of spreadsheets and CSV files and use them to pose and answer questions."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-2",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-3",
      "name": "Working with Data Files",
      "absolute_position": 4,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can programs work with files?**\n\nIn this lesson, students are introduced to the `csv` module for working with CSV files. They use the module to read a CSV file into dictionaries and iterate through the data. They also debug some errors that might occur when reading data from CSV files.",
        "student_overview": "**How can programs work with files?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll learn about and use the `csv` module for working with CSV files. You'll use the module to read a CSV file into dictionaries and iterate through the data."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-3",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-4",
      "name": "Analyzing Categorical Data",
      "absolute_position": 5,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I tell when something unusual happens?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn how to analyze simple categorical data while exploring a common AI use-case: detecting anomalies. Students play a game based on random chance, learning to detect cheaters by finding unlikely distributions of outcomes. They then apply what they’ve learned by writing Python programs that analyze data for anomalies in a variety of contexts.",
        "preparation": "- Print one copy of the Anomaly Detection Activity Guide for each student.",
        "student_overview": "**How can I tell when something unusual happens?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll learn how to analyze simple categorical data while exploring a common AI use-case: detecting anomalies. You'll play a game based on random chance, learning to detect cheaters by finding unlikely distributions of outcomes. You'll then apply what they’ve learned by writing Python programs that analyze data for anomalies in a variety of contexts."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-4",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-5",
      "name": "Analyzing Numerical Data",
      "absolute_position": 6,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can numerical data be analyzed?**\n\nIn this lesson, students develop familiarity with summary statistics—mean, range, and standard deviation—through an unplugged activity where they guess the number of jelly beans in a jar. Students translate their knowledge of these statistics into Python code that helps them compare and make sense of two different datasets.",
        "preparation": "- Print one copy of the Jelly Beans Activity Guide per student. ",
        "student_overview": "**How can numerical data be analyzed?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll develop familiarity with summary statistics—mean, range, and standard deviation—through an unplugged activity where you'll guess the number of jelly beans in a jar. You'll then translate your knowledge of these statistics into Python code that helps you compare and make sense of two different datasets."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-5",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-6",
      "name": "Analyze a Dataset Project",
      "absolute_position": 7,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "In this lesson, students pick a dataset of interest and use their knowledge of data analysis to pose questions about the data that would be useful to answer. Using Python, students write a program that analyzes the data and computes results that can help to answer those questions. Then, students interpret the results of the analysis and write about their connection to the original questions.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Analyze a Dataset Project Planning Guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "In this lesson, you'll pick a dataset of interest and use your knowledge of data analysis to pose questions about the data that would be useful to answer. Using Python, you'll write a program that analyzes the data and computes results that can help to answer those questions. "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-6",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-7",
      "name": "Visualizing Data (Part 1)",
      "absolute_position": 8,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I use code to better see my data?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn how to use `matplotlib`—a Python data visualization library—to visualize categorical and numerical data. They practice modifying existing plots, then create their own plot to visualize their choice of dataset.",
        "preparation": "- Print a few copies of the American Time Use Survey handout, one for each natural group of students in the classroom.",
        "student_overview": "**How can I use code to better see my data?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll learn how to use `matplotlib`—a Python data visualization library—to visualize categorical and numerical data. You'll practice modifying existing plots, then create your own plot to visualize your choice of dataset."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-7",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-8",
      "name": "Visualizing Data (Part 2)",
      "absolute_position": 9,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I design plots that communicate information well?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn about three goals of data visualization—clarity, accessibility, and honesty. Given their choice of dataset, students learn to improve the design of a plot in service of such goals. Along the way, they practice reading documentation to use functions they haven’t yet seen.",
        "preparation": "- Print one double-sided copy of the `matplotlib` Documentation Handout for every three students. Optionally, for easier reference, print one copy for each student. Consider saving these copies for Unit 3, Lesson 12.",
        "student_overview": "**How can I design plots that communicate information well?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll learn about three goals of data visualization—clarity, accessibility, and honesty. Given your choice of dataset, you'll learn to improve the design of a plot in service of such goals. Along the way, you'll practice reading documentation to use functions you haven’t yet seen."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-8",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-9",
      "name": "Telling Stories with Data",
      "absolute_position": 10,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**What kinds of stories can data tell?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore how data can be used as a tool for communication. They are introduced to data journalism as a career and explore how data journalists use data to tell stories and communicate information. Students start by critically evaluating data stories: they look at visualizations, headlines, and their underlying data to assess how well the data is communicated. Students then practice data journalism skills themselves: designing visualizations and writing headlines that communicate information from a dataset.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Data Journalism Activity Guide (one for each pair of students).",
        "student_overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**What kinds of stories can data tell?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll explore how data can be used as a tool for communication. You'll explore how data journalists use data to tell stories and communicate information. You'll  critically evaluating data stories and practice data journalism skills with your own visualizations and headlines.",
        "unplugged": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-9",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-10",
      "name": "Collecting Data",
      "absolute_position": 11,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**Where does data come from?**\n\nIn this lesson, students investigate a research question by deciding how to measure the variables it describes and collecting data that can answer it. Students first evaluate several sampling strategies, considering who each one includes and excludes, and then design survey questions of their own.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Sampling Strategies Activity Guide (one for each pair of students). ",
        "student_overview": "**Where does data come from?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll investigate a research question by deciding how to measure the variables it describes and collecting data that can answer it. You'll evaluate several sampling strategies, considering who each one includes and excludes, and then design survey questions of your own."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-10",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-11",
      "name": "Preparing Data",
      "absolute_position": 12,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**What strategies can help to deal with messy data?**\n\nIn this lesson, students take on the role of data archaeologists restoring the records of a lost 1937 expedition whose three logbooks disagree with one another. Students first attempt to answer a question using printed logbook pages and discover firsthand why damaged, inconsistent, and scattered records resist analysis. After defining data cleaning and writing a plain-English restoration plan, students implement the plan in Python: they filter out unreadable rows, standardize inconsistent site names, and combine records from three sources. The restored archive reveals an answer that no single logbook could provide.",
        "preparation": "- Print a copy of the Restoring the Archive Activity Guide for each student.",
        "student_overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**What strategies can help to deal with messy data?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll take on the role of a data archaeologist restoring the records of a lost 1937 expedition whose three logbooks disagree with one another."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-11",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-12",
      "name": "Data Research Project",
      "absolute_position": 13,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "In this project, students ask and answer a research question about the relationship between two variables of their choice. They design a survey, gather real data from their classmates, and create their own visualization—ultimately telling a story that answers their original question. Along the way, students apply what they've learned throughout the unit: how to ethically collect, honestly visualize, and compellingly communicate data.",
        "preparation": "- Print one copy of the Data Research Project Planning Guide for each student.\n- Print one double-sided copy of the `matplotlib` Documentation Handout for every three students. Or, re-use copies from Unit 3, Lesson 8.\n",
        "student_overview": "In this project, you'll ask and answer a research question about the relationship between two variables of your choice. You'll design a survey, gather real data from your classmates, and create your own visualization—ultimately telling a story that answers your original question."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-12",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-14",
