  ],
  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "c1d1af35-f84c-4475-abfd-2cf92cb045ac",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Pre-Assesssment"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c1d1af35-f84c-4475-abfd-2cf92cb045ac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ac56e68c-82fc-4443-aed0-3392e0fc8bda"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "59613137-9701-4efa-be10-637df99e8647",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** As students enter the room, prompt them to collect the materials they’ll  need to play *Candidate*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce this lesson’s career connection: a Data Analyst.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "59613137-9701-4efa-be10-637df99e8647",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a58d91f-18c1-4ade-8812-cfdf2624189a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c56b5523-c8d8-4dcd-8181-623ac86d78fe",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce students to the data analysis process by playing Candidate. In *Candidate*, students ask strategic questions, collect simple data, and interpret it to run for student government on the most popular policy.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to *Candidate*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the premise and goals of *Candidate*.",
        "duration": "25",
        "name": "Candidate"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c56b5523-c8d8-4dcd-8181-623ac86d78fe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7ef77f4a-5182-487c-83ba-ec7304f1a148",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You are running for student government. Your goal is to choose the most popular policy. There are five policies to choose from. Let’s see them.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7ef77f4a-5182-487c-83ba-ec7304f1a148",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5506fa1a-87fc-4217-8e72-6b754e069fcc",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal and read aloud the five policy choices.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the voting mechanics."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5506fa1a-87fc-4217-8e72-6b754e069fcc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6ca7dde9-686c-461b-b1da-804d537fbff2",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Look at your Persona Card. Keep it a secret from your neighbor! The icon in the top right is the policy your persona will vote for at the end of the game. You still have to choose your own policy to run on: the policy your persona will vote for isn’t the policy you have to choose.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6ca7dde9-686c-461b-b1da-804d537fbff2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8fdeb71e-776b-41aa-9901-15e8d86a9e77",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the win condition: students need to pick the policy that is most popular among the class’s persona cards."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8fdeb71e-776b-41aa-9901-15e8d86a9e77",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "44341363-edbe-4419-ac36-62ae8cbe3d8d",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "At the end of the game, we’ll tally up the Persona Card votes to see which policy is the most popular. If you choose the policy that’s most popular among all of the class’s personas, you’ll win!",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Consider being extra clear: students themselves—not their Persona Cards—are choosing a policy to run on. Students can choose to run on a policy other than the policy their persona card will vote for. And their Persona Cards—not students themselves—are voting on policies to determine each policy’s popularity."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "44341363-edbe-4419-ac36-62ae8cbe3d8d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ec868725-c7f5-4ca6-9203-24ef8c46286b",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the rounds of *Candidate*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ec868725-c7f5-4ca6-9203-24ef8c46286b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eca43d69-1719-4dd4-bb5a-a3ef3a5769c3",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "How should you choose a policy to run on? Ask the personas! We’ll play the game in two rounds: in each round, you’ll ask one yes-or-no question of the personas in your group.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eca43d69-1719-4dd4-bb5a-a3ef3a5769c3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d75f49af-0d53-4718-8e22-a26e8100bbac",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "With five policies and only two rounds of questions, you won’t be able to ask about each policy. You’ll need to get creative with your questions to gather as much information as you can.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d75f49af-0d53-4718-8e22-a26e8100bbac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1b5c6b62-1b8a-406c-a1cd-46f7f2892cbe",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to get into groups to begin playing *Candidate*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Step students through Round 1 of *Candidate*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *data analysis*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1b5c6b62-1b8a-406c-a1cd-46f7f2892cbe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "81dbf808-f04c-4c76-9dc3-9946de58bc11",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Data analysis is exactly what you’ll do first-hand today. Your job is to ask questions, collect data from personas, and use that information to make a call about which policy you’ll run on.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "81dbf808-f04c-4c76-9dc3-9946de58bc11",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9df665e9-1685-4f03-99d7-5cb4865c3802",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to write a yes-or-no question on their Activity Guide, then pass their Activity Guide to their left. Once all students have done so, reveal the next instructions."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9df665e9-1685-4f03-99d7-5cb4865c3802",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2fed8586-909c-4515-8e6b-5d2a7ef89549",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Give your question some thought. Make sure it’s a question you think other personas can answer. And try to write a question that gives you information about more than one policy’s possible popularity.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2fed8586-909c-4515-8e6b-5d2a7ef89549",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "164d5221-54cb-40bd-b9bc-25914eefce50",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students write, look for questions that can’t be answered with a yes or a no. Remind those students that the question must be a yes-or-no question.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "While students think, consider displaying the five policies again—on a previous slide—for their reference. They can also reference the policies on their Activity Guide."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "164d5221-54cb-40bd-b9bc-25914eefce50",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0e1c6cdb-4055-4686-8265-9acf3b6df49e",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to read the question they’ve received. Then, prompt them to answer the question based on how their Persona Card would answer it. Finally, prompt them to continue passing Activity Guides and answering questions until they receive their own Activity Guide back."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0e1c6cdb-4055-4686-8265-9acf3b6df49e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "114f051e-6056-4e82-b1dd-f1bf8b3e7e31",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Read the question you’ve received. Then read your Persona Card. In the next row, put a check in the \"Yes\" or \"No\" column, based on the information on your Persona Card. If you’re really unsure about how to answer, or can’t understand the question, place a check in the \"Unsure\" column. Now, repeat until you get your own Activity Guide back.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "114f051e-6056-4e82-b1dd-f1bf8b3e7e31",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "59ea0205-8002-4a44-a7cd-895a6a09f163",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students pass their Activity Guides, look for…\n- Readers that slow a group down. If students get stuck, consider helping them read the question—or advise them to ask the student who wrote the question.\n- Students answering a question as themselves, rather than their Persona Card. Remind them that they should reference their Persona Card to answer each question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to reflect on what they’ve learned from their question."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "59ea0205-8002-4a44-a7cd-895a6a09f163",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "297ec75d-da48-4557-8b0c-277a9dd326df",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now it’s time to analyze the data you’ve collected. Respond to the question in your Activity Guide. As you respond, think about what you now know about the personas in the classroom. Which policies do you think will be more popular than others?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "297ec75d-da48-4557-8b0c-277a9dd326df",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0f3086fd-acc6-4eed-951d-a3da98e511df",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Step students through Round 2 of Candidate."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0f3086fd-acc6-4eed-951d-a3da98e511df",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "340f78da-92cf-4e9d-83de-384d93c1ec09",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Before we begin Round 2, let’s reflect.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "340f78da-92cf-4e9d-83de-384d93c1ec09",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "78257f37-b4f6-4e0b-b6c3-8ea9e412a655",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What do you think makes a good question? Can you share any examples you’ve seen?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to reflect on how to write a strong question. Strong responses will describe questions that surface a common theme among multiple policies. For example, it might be effective to ask \"Do you have a lot to do after school?\", because multiple policies relate to after-school scheduling. Make the point that questions which touch on common themes, rather than questions that ask about specific policies, are more effective when you can only ask a few questions. Once students have shared a few examples they’ve seen, continue with Round 2.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to write a yes-or-no question on their Activity Guide, then pass their Activity Guide to their left. Once all students have done so, reveal the next instructions."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "78257f37-b4f6-4e0b-b6c3-8ea9e412a655",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "61beedf4-6251-48ce-a429-c92b61a79175",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Again, give your next question some thought. Make sure it’s a question you think other personas can answer.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "61beedf4-6251-48ce-a429-c92b61a79175",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "49e8fcf7-2f65-4669-906c-1a0af87445c8",
      "position": 23,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students write, look again for questions that can’t be answered with a yes or a no. Remind those students that the question must be a yes-or-no question.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "While students think, consider displaying the five policies again—on a previous slide—for their reference. They can also reference the policies on their Activity Guide."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "49e8fcf7-2f65-4669-906c-1a0af87445c8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "69f25247-b6bc-4688-a7de-48ce6af62855",
      "position": 24,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to read the question they’ve received. Then, prompt them to answer the question based on how their Persona Card would answer it. Finally, prompt them to continue passing Activity Guides and answering questions until they receive their own Activity Guide back."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "69f25247-b6bc-4688-a7de-48ce6af62855",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "47d69c0a-3182-4f20-af05-e9bf1c11782a",
      "position": 25,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Just like Round 1. Read the question you’ve received. Then read your Persona Card. In the next row, put a check in the \"Yes\" or \"No\" column, based on the information on your Persona Card. If you’re really unsure about how to answer, or can’t understand the question, place a check in the \"Unsure\" column. Now, repeat until you get your own Activity Guide back.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "47d69c0a-3182-4f20-af05-e9bf1c11782a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "57a74983-c887-45e4-8dd5-c084e23ea339",
      "position": 26,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students pass their Activity Guides, look again for…\n- Readers that slow a group down. If students get stuck, consider helping them read the question—or advise them to ask the student who wrote the question.\n- Students answering a question as themselves, rather than their Persona Card. Remind them that they should reference their Persona Card to answer each question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to reflect on what they’ve learned from their question."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "57a74983-c887-45e4-8dd5-c084e23ea339",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eafcc623-4d37-48ba-ab2c-2f1fce157fdd",
      "position": 27,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now you have two questions whose data you can analyze. Using what you’ve learned from the data, decide on the policy you think will be most popular.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Consider being particularly explicit that students should choose the policy they believe will be most popular based on the data they’ve collected—not the policy that they personally want."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eafcc623-4d37-48ba-ab2c-2f1fce157fdd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bdcd147f-6145-4136-aae8-63aa9770cfd6",
      "position": 28,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Start to reveal the results of the game.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the five possible policy choices. Tell students it’s time to reveal which policy they’re going to run on. Name each policy, and ask students to raise their hand or shout out if they are running on that policy.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Name each policy again. As you do, tell students to raise their hand if their persona is voting for that policy. Keep track of each policy’s popularity by writing the number of votes for each policy on the board.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Once the results are in, celebrate! Students who picked the most popular policy are the \"winners,\" though remind students the main point of the game is to see the data analysis process first-hand.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If you have additional time, consider asking students: \"How was this game different from how real elections work?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bdcd147f-6145-4136-aae8-63aa9770cfd6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e04f519c-235d-4c77-8950-46c2da05d0f0",
      "position": 29,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Create time for students to reflect on their early experience with the data analysis process.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Reflecting on Candidate"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e04f519c-235d-4c77-8950-46c2da05d0f0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "862848cc-f69a-474a-a962-d587a1a8a1a8",
      "position": 30,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Question of the Day"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "862848cc-f69a-474a-a962-d587a1a8a1a8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "883966e7-a51a-40d4-bd3c-b9a9c19c3334",
      "position": 31,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 1. Students answer the Question of the Day. After they do, discuss a related question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Describe your process for deciding on a question and analyzing the data you collected. What did you learn?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Draw out students’ experiences with the data analysis process. Emphasize it’s not expected students will have expertly handled the process: they may have chosen a question that didn’t work or received data that wasn’t very useful. Rather, it’s expected students demonstrate they’ve learned something about, or from, the process: that data analysis involves choosing questions carefully, collecting data, and trying to make sense of it—even if it doesn’t always lead to direct answers."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "883966e7-a51a-40d4-bd3c-b9a9c19c3334",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9cc77f36-55dd-4f63-9a74-486ee7099b66",
      "position": 32,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Even if you didn’t win today’s game, you still learned something important about data analysis. Data analysis is about choosing good questions, collecting relevant data, and trying to make sense of that data. As we continue in this unit, we’ll explore the data analysis process in more depth. We’ll see why and how data analysis is an important part of building AI systems.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9cc77f36-55dd-4f63-9a74-486ee7099b66",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0fe2b91a-b601-4fa9-9718-a5be98cbdb6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8303506f-5ad7-46ea-ba67-34f601e85eab",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8303506f-5ad7-46ea-ba67-34f601e85eab",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a98eb46-28c3-487a-a811-eff6d70ee779"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0cbfc344-96ce-405a-bf06-8933e5032536",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the scenario for the first part of the class: a school is collecting data to better understand their buses and how to improve them.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give each student a blank sheet of paper."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0cbfc344-96ce-405a-bf06-8933e5032536",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fd2eec0c-3d71-4b17-a09f-a35114543dcd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8815ab6a-0486-4104-985d-f9769734b9e7",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "On the next slide, you’re going to see some information about the buses. Your job will be to write down and organize the information on your sheet of paper. You can organize it however it makes the most sense to you!\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8815ab6a-0486-4104-985d-f9769734b9e7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fd2eec0c-3d71-4b17-a09f-a35114543dcd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f9113442-b8dc-4b5f-97a3-3d681f2d0218",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask for questions about the activity before proceeding to the next slide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students a few minutes to read the slide and organize the data on their own sheet of paper.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students are unsure how to organize the data, remind them that there isn’t any one correct answer. Students can organize the data however it makes the most sense to them. If students are stuck, encourage them to think about organizing the data in a way that makes it easy to read and answer questions about the data later.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f9113442-b8dc-4b5f-97a3-3d681f2d0218",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fd2eec0c-3d71-4b17-a09f-a35114543dcd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "786d57a9-50df-4d95-8ba3-bd9abacd5a6e",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Why did you choose to organize data the way you did?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students discuss how they organized the bus ride data. There isn’t one correct answer, but strong responses should explain how they chose to represent the information and offer reasoning for why the representation is useful from the perspective of reading or using the data later. For example, some students might have organized the data as a table with one row per bus, while others might have organized the data with one row per day. Those students might justify their decisions by pointing out the kinds of questions that can be answered more easily (for example: “my table makes it easy to compare the two buses with each other”). Some students might not have used a table at all: for example, students might have created a bulleted list or a graphical visualization."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "786d57a9-50df-4d95-8ba3-bd9abacd5a6e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fd2eec0c-3d71-4b17-a09f-a35114543dcd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d4dbdd0b-7f27-4155-a101-30b196a4c0e7",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "How we choose to organize data makes a difference for how easy it is to understand and use the data.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d4dbdd0b-7f27-4155-a101-30b196a4c0e7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fd2eec0c-3d71-4b17-a09f-a35114543dcd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fe975f88-e10d-429f-be99-e5b67a0c280f",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fe975f88-e10d-429f-be99-e5b67a0c280f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fd2eec0c-3d71-4b17-a09f-a35114543dcd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c00a021b-3fd7-4e2b-97f8-de882371166a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _tabular data_.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Exploring Tabular Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c00a021b-3fd7-4e2b-97f8-de882371166a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9005e845-1e91-46ae-a7c7-44d932fe5bf8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9275d034-d63f-45f5-b536-2d3ebef407c6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Organizing data into rows and columns can make it easier to access and understand information.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9275d034-d63f-45f5-b536-2d3ebef407c6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9005e845-1e91-46ae-a7c7-44d932fe5bf8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7a3bfc59-0ea6-4b73-9300-28df21256f42",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the example on the slide of one possible way of organizing the bus data. Explain that each row represents one entry of data: for example, in this case, each row represents a bus journey on a particular date. Explain that each column represents specific pieces of information about each entry of data: for example, how far the bus traveled or how many riders the bus carried could be stored in columns.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students ask about other possible ways of organizing the bus data in a table, acknowledge that other options are possible. For example, some students might have chosen to have one row per day of the week, with columns representing different buses that traveled on those days.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7a3bfc59-0ea6-4b73-9300-28df21256f42",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9005e845-1e91-46ae-a7c7-44d932fe5bf8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e20977e7-2dd9-4c04-a2aa-3b0b7d2356ae",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Exploring Tabular Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e20977e7-2dd9-4c04-a2aa-3b0b7d2356ae",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9005e845-1e91-46ae-a7c7-44d932fe5bf8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d8f8f671-5db4-4a4a-bff7-e73d5056c0f9",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 1.\n- Level 1: Students choose a tabular dataset to explore. They answer questions about the dataset and then pose new questions of their own.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look and listen for common struggles with navigating and interpreting tabular data.\n- Some students might struggle to understand what each number in the dataset represents. Point students towards the headings at the top of each column to help them interpret the meaning of numbers in the dataset.\n- Some students might not know where to start looking for answers or might be overwhelmed by the amount of data present in the dataset. Help these students by encouraging them to focus on one row at a time. For example, if a question specifically pertains to data on Mondays, you might help students identify the column of the dataset that relates to dates and work with students to identify which rows are relevant to answering the question and which rows aren’t relevant.\n- For students who struggle with the final question about questions that couldn’t be answered by the dataset, encourage students to think creatively. For example, you might ask “If you were collecting this data yourself, what other information would be helpful to gather?”\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "To help students validate that their answers are correct, you can encourage students to work in pairs, or you can have students check their answers with each other after they’ve worked through the level.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d8f8f671-5db4-4a4a-bff7-e73d5056c0f9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9005e845-1e91-46ae-a7c7-44d932fe5bf8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "417a0ff7-08a8-4b0c-9b0b-b3703b6128e4",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Computers store information inside of files. For example, when you’ve created your own Python programs, you’ve written your code inside of a file.\n\nIf we want computers to work with tabular data, it’s helpful to organize data in a text file that’s easy for computers to read.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Creating CSV Files",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "417a0ff7-08a8-4b0c-9b0b-b3703b6128e4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9005e845-1e91-46ae-a7c7-44d932fe5bf8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6ed875a6-de38-4da6-aaea-479a9650d2a7",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the text representation to the left as a way of representing the data to the right in a text file.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Is this a good way to store a table in a text file? Why or why not?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students discuss the tradeoffs of organizing tabular data in text form with one line per row and cells aligned vertically with spaces. Strong responses might mention strengths, weaknesses, or both. For example, students might point out the strength that the format is easy to read: it’s easy to look at the text file and see what the rows and columns are. There are several weaknesses of this representation that students might notice as well. One weakness is that different lines need different amounts of space: you need to include more space after “Monday” than after “Tuesday”. Another weakness is that adding data might require reformatting other lines: for example, adding a new row for “Wednesday” might require adding extra spaces to all other lines to make enough room for the longer word “Wednesday.” A subtler weakness is that the format won’t handle spaces in the data well: for example, if a column name was “Fuel Used”, it might not be clear whether “Fuel Used” is one column or two different columns. If students struggle to identify these weaknesses, prompt them with these scenarios (for example, you might ask “What would need to happen to add a new row for Wednesday?”) and encourage them to think about the consequences.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6ed875a6-de38-4da6-aaea-479a9650d2a7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9005e845-1e91-46ae-a7c7-44d932fe5bf8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "333d2175-13ce-4b8b-98b0-f641079ae70d",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Using multiple spaces to line up data is easy for humans to look at, but it can be harder for a computer to work with.\n\nInstead, when we store tables in a computer, we’ll often use commas to separate pieces of data.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "333d2175-13ce-4b8b-98b0-f641079ae70d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9005e845-1e91-46ae-a7c7-44d932fe5bf8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a739f965-c5a1-4bfe-b52f-e760569de974",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain this representation of data. Show that each row is still represented by one line of text. Explain that within each row, a comma is used to separate values in different columns of the table.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a739f965-c5a1-4bfe-b52f-e760569de974",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9005e845-1e91-46ae-a7c7-44d932fe5bf8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "73a206da-e04f-4874-a7e5-e357991df1ec",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This way of representing data might be a little harder for humans to read, but it’s much easier for a computer to work with.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "73a206da-e04f-4874-a7e5-e357991df1ec",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9005e845-1e91-46ae-a7c7-44d932fe5bf8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e21be276-e016-4d22-a35d-3fa084e9ffa3",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _CSV file_."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e21be276-e016-4d22-a35d-3fa084e9ffa3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9005e845-1e91-46ae-a7c7-44d932fe5bf8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b8c999c0-0956-48cb-8573-b1b8e2d217ea",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "CSV stands for “comma-separated values.” In a CSV file, we use commas to separate different values in a row. Computers can look for where the line breaks and commas are to tell what data belongs in separate rows or columns.\n\nToday, you’ll practice writing CSV files to get used to how they organize data.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b8c999c0-0956-48cb-8573-b1b8e2d217ea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9005e845-1e91-46ae-a7c7-44d932fe5bf8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8be4455d-0118-4ca7-931d-e97d0599aaed",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Creating a CSV File"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8be4455d-0118-4ca7-931d-e97d0599aaed",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9005e845-1e91-46ae-a7c7-44d932fe5bf8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "558ae6c1-02f9-4c66-b2f1-13348c0b3875",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Levels 2–3.\n- Level 2: Students pick a dataset and convert information from a spreadsheet to a CSV file.\n- Level 3: Students complete a Check Your Understanding question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, help students with the process of translating their spreadsheet to a CSV file.\n- Help students construct rows of data. Remind them of the CSV file format and help them use commas to separate pieces of information.\n- If students aren’t sure about how to populate the header row, encourage them to look at the spreadsheet and determine what columns exist. The first line of student’s CSV file should reflect those columns, with commas separating the column names.\n- Encourage students to press “Run” within Python Lab. Though students don’t write any Python code of their own in these levels, running the Python Lab will still visualize students’ CSV files in a way that might be easier to interpret. Visualizing the CSV files can also be helpful for identifying bugs like missing commas.\n- Remind students that they don’t need to translate the entire spreadsheet to a CSV file: they only need to add at least three rows of data to their CSV file.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 3 as an opportunity to assess students’ comfort with representing tabular data in CSV files. Students take the perspective of a computer and explain how a computer would know how to interpret CSV-formatted data. If students struggle to identify how to distinguish rows and columns, show students the example of a CSV file again and remind students about the role of new lines and commas in organizing data.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "558ae6c1-02f9-4c66-b2f1-13348c0b3875",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9005e845-1e91-46ae-a7c7-44d932fe5bf8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cfa507f0-9768-438b-adfb-7f7712b7466c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cfa507f0-9768-438b-adfb-7f7712b7466c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "320433c9-e9f7-424f-93dd-a51e7798f2f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b8479cc8-508f-4f96-bf7e-a96d5054f1aa",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Describe how in the early days of computing, data was represented using physical punch cards. Each card had to be manually punched and could only represent a small amount of data."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b8479cc8-508f-4f96-bf7e-a96d5054f1aa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2493120f-5275-4a1a-9311-889a9ba48ac2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cb72df8c-5b36-497a-8c78-cc316d35d142",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In order to get the data into the computer, you had to feed the cards into the computer one by one. One dataset could be thousands of cards – so this could take a long time!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cb72df8c-5b36-497a-8c78-cc316d35d142",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2493120f-5275-4a1a-9311-889a9ba48ac2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "534ac8cc-6c06-4feb-aa5a-e0ff68808163",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that modern computers can have files that represent information, like a table of structured data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What can we do with data once we feed it into a computer program?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students surface intuitions about what programs can do with data. Strong responses will consider possible applications of data in software, such as training machine learning models with data. If students are struggling, prompt them to think about ways they’ve used data so far already in the course.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "534ac8cc-6c06-4feb-aa5a-e0ff68808163",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2493120f-5275-4a1a-9311-889a9ba48ac2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2538f073-245a-4369-b5d1-ab61a96816e6",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students are introduced to using the `csv` module as a tool to read data from CSV files in Python.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Introducing the CSV Module"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2538f073-245a-4369-b5d1-ab61a96816e6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "98adeea3-e259-4389-a1f7-61b6276211b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5dff81a6-ec6d-416b-910d-199dac849d35",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Python has a CSV module that makes it easier for us to read CSV files into our programs and work with them.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5dff81a6-ec6d-416b-910d-199dac849d35",
        "lesson_activity.key": "98adeea3-e259-4389-a1f7-61b6276211b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "05c6ec86-5862-4b05-a2d1-c371090ff835",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Progress through the next slides to walk through the code for reading from a CSV file.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "05c6ec86-5862-4b05-a2d1-c371090ff835",
        "lesson_activity.key": "98adeea3-e259-4389-a1f7-61b6276211b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9add1ccd-6d3b-4d1e-9033-d907012b920a",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "First, we import the `csv` module so that we can use its functions.\n\nThen, we “open” our file. The value we pass to the `open` function is the file path.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9add1ccd-6d3b-4d1e-9033-d907012b920a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "98adeea3-e259-4389-a1f7-61b6276211b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1774bb99-6d6c-47dc-9636-da59b4441058",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _file path_."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1774bb99-6d6c-47dc-9636-da59b4441058",
        "lesson_activity.key": "98adeea3-e259-4389-a1f7-61b6276211b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c81693f8-2ab9-410c-bdc4-3d6da109abb2",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here, the file path is “table.csv” which tells the program the name of the file to open.\n\nIf the file lived inside a folder called “data”, the file path would look like this. This slash tells the program to look inside the folder called “data” to find the file.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Pause to see if students have any questions about file paths.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c81693f8-2ab9-410c-bdc4-3d6da109abb2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "98adeea3-e259-4389-a1f7-61b6276211b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4d36e7cf-be69-4c7e-b7ce-983252a0e3ae",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Then, the `csv` module lets us create a `DictReader` object. A `DictReader` object lets us read a CSV file one line at a time, where each line is turned into a Python dictionary.\n\nWhat do you think these lines of code do?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4d36e7cf-be69-4c7e-b7ce-983252a0e3ae",
        "lesson_activity.key": "98adeea3-e259-4389-a1f7-61b6276211b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8aeec373-489e-40a3-9602-62aa9b94b8b4",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Let students suggest what they think lines 4-6 will do."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8aeec373-489e-40a3-9602-62aa9b94b8b4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "98adeea3-e259-4389-a1f7-61b6276211b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3ff8499c-7adb-4986-9dc7-5784dd0dad37",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Once we have a `DictReader`, we can use a `for` loop to loop over each line of the CSV file. The `DictReader` turns each line into a dictionary that we can use in the ways we've used dictionaries before!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3ff8499c-7adb-4986-9dc7-5784dd0dad37",
        "lesson_activity.key": "98adeea3-e259-4389-a1f7-61b6276211b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "21d24cbd-d529-44d0-b2ba-3e95f14d2037",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point out to students that the keys in the dictionary will be the names of the columns of the CSV file. In this example, the CSV file has columns named “age” and “height”.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Pause to see if students have any questions about using the `csv` module to read a CSV file as dictionaries."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "21d24cbd-d529-44d0-b2ba-3e95f14d2037",
        "lesson_activity.key": "98adeea3-e259-4389-a1f7-61b6276211b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "197e9f88-77fb-4373-95f4-927b672766de",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When we’re done using our file, we should close it. This lets Python know we’re done using the file.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "197e9f88-77fb-4373-95f4-927b672766de",
        "lesson_activity.key": "98adeea3-e259-4389-a1f7-61b6276211b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "946fb34b-2377-436f-a1b1-52009b48464b",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students get experience using Python to read csv data into programs.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Reading Data with Python"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "946fb34b-2377-436f-a1b1-52009b48464b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "98adeea3-e259-4389-a1f7-61b6276211b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "349a7288-41a2-4792-97f7-9e889c341b02",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now, you’ll have a chance to practice writing code that reads CSV data files.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "349a7288-41a2-4792-97f7-9e889c341b02",
        "lesson_activity.key": "98adeea3-e259-4389-a1f7-61b6276211b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "91c9d756-acd4-4007-a507-efab0c40f707",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Working with CSV Files"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "91c9d756-acd4-4007-a507-efab0c40f707",
        "lesson_activity.key": "98adeea3-e259-4389-a1f7-61b6276211b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "28619abd-1375-470c-bbd7-80c9ea579db1",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to levels 1-2.\n- Level 1: Students write code to read data from a CSV file and print values from the file.\n- Level 2: Students debug a program that is not reading a CSV file correctly. The first error is that the file path is incorrect, and the second error is that the CSV file is missing a comma in between the column names.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work on the levels, keep an eye out for common struggles.\n- Some students might struggle with correctly importing the `csv` module: help those students make sure that the `import` statement is at the top of their file.\n- Students might struggle with the order of logic for working with CSV files: help students make sure they open the file before passing it to the `DictReader`.\n- Students might have trouble with writing a loop to read the file: remind them about the syntax for looping and accessing dictionary values.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "28619abd-1375-470c-bbd7-80c9ea579db1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "98adeea3-e259-4389-a1f7-61b6276211b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "35a2584d-964c-430d-b126-14a9839cde5d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "35a2584d-964c-430d-b126-14a9839cde5d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a796a784-2bc0-47f4-92aa-90d2fb3be5e9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6dc52792-3e94-4b3a-81cb-696da40c968e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *In some games, players roll a 20-sided die. Higher numbers are better. How many times in a row could someone roll a 20 before you’d get suspicious? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Gather students’ intuitions about this lesson’s theme: anomaly detection. Strong answers will say they’d be suspicious after 4 or so rolls, because the odds of doing so are very low. Once you’ve surveyed a few students’ opinions, continue on.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6dc52792-3e94-4b3a-81cb-696da40c968e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a9cf3996-32f4-4ccb-9c83-83591be6697e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ecd6dd66-fc55-4f02-8663-627ad0bb729b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce students to frequency distributions by inviting them to play a simple game—*Flip*. In *Flip*, students flip a coin—receiving either 1 point for heads or 0 points for tails—and continue flipping 20 times total. The student with the most points after 20 flips wins!\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Lead students in playing *Flip* as a whole class. Consider synchronizing everyone’s coin flips so that suspense builds and the game concludes at the same time for everyone.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Playing Flip"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ecd6dd66-fc55-4f02-8663-627ad0bb729b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cb0fe1d6-cd02-4a0f-b0fd-aea54e9696b2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d271f144-53ef-4ab1-99b2-f0e2746609e7",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Playing Flip"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d271f144-53ef-4ab1-99b2-f0e2746609e7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cb0fe1d6-cd02-4a0f-b0fd-aea54e9696b2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3d876abd-c4ba-43af-85f3-419db5235676",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4, Level 1.\n- **Level 1:** Students play *Flip* together.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students play *Flip*, note students who happened to have particularly even distributions (i.e., an equal number of heads and tails) and students who happened to have particularly skewed distributions (i.e., significantly more heads than tails, or vice versa). Consider calling on those students in the following discussion.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *In your 20 flips, how many heads did you get? How many tails?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to share their frequency distributions—without yet defining them as such—from playing *Flip*. Make note of students who had particularly even distributions (i.e., a roughly equal number of heads and tails). Point out that these students’ distributions are somewhat expected: over many coin flips, a fair coin should show roughly an equal number of heads and tails. Also make note of any students who had particularly skewed distributions (i.e., significantly more heads than tails, or vice versa). Point out that these students’ distributions are somewhat unexpected: it’s rare, over many coin flips, for a coin to present one side significantly more than another.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define frequency distribution. Point to the visual students saw while playing *Flip* as an example of a frequency distribution. Explain that coin flips from *Flip* fall into one of two categories: the coin lands on heads, or the coin lands on tails. By counting the number of times a coin flip does each, they create a frequency distribution."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3d876abd-c4ba-43af-85f3-419db5235676",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cb0fe1d6-cd02-4a0f-b0fd-aea54e9696b2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "59de4d11-d41f-4da1-a20b-f076a5d486ea",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We’ve seen an example of a frequency distribution with two categories. But a frequency distribution can have more than two categories, and often does!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "59de4d11-d41f-4da1-a20b-f076a5d486ea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cb0fe1d6-cd02-4a0f-b0fd-aea54e9696b2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "15c7f86d-fef9-4298-a3da-0dd67743c285",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Could you tell if your Flip opponent is cheating? Let’s find out!",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Anomaly Detection",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "15c7f86d-fef9-4298-a3da-0dd67743c285",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9335b099-9c14-4cd9-b5e4-2bfcf68dbdd2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e0f455fd-1ec9-48a9-92ff-c5d2d1c4af05",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Anomaly Detection Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work on the Activity Guide, look to offer guidance on how to create a frequency distribution for both Alice and Bob. Consider drawing an example on the board, so that students have a reference to work from.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Is Alice cheating?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to share their thinking from the Activity Guide. Particularly strong claims will make use of the probabilistic reasoning students were introduced to in Unit 2: for example, \"It seems likely that Alice cheated, but there’s a small chance she didn’t.\" Look for students to leverage what they learned from the prior discussion—that some frequency distributions are rare—and to make the leap towards reasoning that if Alice’s frequency distribution is very rare, she might have cheated. If students struggle to answer this question, draw their attention back to the takeaways of the prior discussion: for example, \"Most often, we expect a player to have a roughly equal number of heads and tails. But Alice didn’t—what could that mean?\" Aim to guide students toward a probabilistic answer to this question. Once you’ve done so, continue on."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e0f455fd-1ec9-48a9-92ff-c5d2d1c4af05",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9335b099-9c14-4cd9-b5e4-2bfcf68dbdd2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0871735a-7e6f-4210-bc42-73bfc69234b4",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "What we’ve just discussed is an example of a real-world use of AI: anomaly detection.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0871735a-7e6f-4210-bc42-73bfc69234b4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9335b099-9c14-4cd9-b5e4-2bfcf68dbdd2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1f336643-0877-4adb-9612-e16e9a4aab3a",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *anomaly detection*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1f336643-0877-4adb-9612-e16e9a4aab3a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9335b099-9c14-4cd9-b5e4-2bfcf68dbdd2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a74c36f9-248d-4ae7-8f59-b536469b4dda",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In the same way we noticed Alice’s results were unexpected, anomaly detection is about finding unexpected events in data—and inferring what they might mean.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a74c36f9-248d-4ae7-8f59-b536469b4dda",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9335b099-9c14-4cd9-b5e4-2bfcf68dbdd2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "85d16b8a-fbd6-4aac-a079-958c3332a4a7",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce students to additional examples of anomaly detection, while providing them an opportunity to practice analyzing categorical data in Python code.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Anomaly Detection in Code"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "85d16b8a-fbd6-4aac-a079-958c3332a4a7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9335b099-9c14-4cd9-b5e4-2bfcf68dbdd2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d3494219-e46c-4056-9b50-63c207f70f52",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Anomaly Detection in Code"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d3494219-e46c-4056-9b50-63c207f70f52",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9335b099-9c14-4cd9-b5e4-2bfcf68dbdd2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ff3a984c-aac2-4768-87c6-183c393d1abb",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4, Levels 2–3.\n- **Level 2:** Students use Python to analyze their choice of data set for anomalies.\n- **Level 3:** Students use their analysis from Level 2 to make a claim about the data.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 3 as an assessment opportunity. Look for students to make a claim about the data that uses the terms they’ve learned in this lesson (e.g., \"anomalous,\" \"distribution\") and to reason probabilistically (e.g., \"It’s likely that x happened\")."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ff3a984c-aac2-4768-87c6-183c393d1abb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9335b099-9c14-4cd9-b5e4-2bfcf68dbdd2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d131bc06-00a1-498b-bf36-1fc8e398651e",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, consider…\n- Reminding students how to access data in a CSV file while using `csv.DictReader`.\n- Helping students interpret the data they’re analyzing within its context. For example, help students understand why it might be a problem if a factory’s inspection process never fails any components."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d131bc06-00a1-498b-bf36-1fc8e398651e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9335b099-9c14-4cd9-b5e4-2bfcf68dbdd2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5ad35442-07b5-40e6-90cd-d007eb2daf4d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5ad35442-07b5-40e6-90cd-d007eb2daf4d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4e6d14b5-566e-4ae3-86d7-7b3282024b99"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ffdcc5e3-1b15-48d1-b5f7-307f8626bcbe",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** As students enter the room, prompt students to form groups.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Jelly Beans Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct the students to make a guess and write it down in their Activity Guides.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the question of the day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ffdcc5e3-1b15-48d1-b5f7-307f8626bcbe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3717975f-d494-4ce7-a748-f3fffb0b0f0b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "60a09024-602b-468b-a03e-8e6c6000e9c1",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce students to mean and range by guiding them in calculating these statistics with their group’s jelly bean guesses. Two groups will win the jelly bean activity: the group with the mean that’s closest to the actual value, and the group with the smallest range.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the objectives of the activity.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Analyzing Jelly Bean Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "60a09024-602b-468b-a03e-8e6c6000e9c1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7689fe1c-843f-42e4-bf17-453242a8afd3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3418a3f9-fe50-4c90-a1fe-8a73d09dd655",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In your groups, you will collect the data on your guesses and analyze it so that we can pick two winners: the group with the closest guess and the group with the most similar guesses. To know which group wins, we’ll need to calculate some statistics.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3418a3f9-fe50-4c90-a1fe-8a73d09dd655",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7689fe1c-843f-42e4-bf17-453242a8afd3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cc44b806-0696-46b2-9508-c3ff4efc8e47",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *statistic*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cc44b806-0696-46b2-9508-c3ff4efc8e47",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7689fe1c-843f-42e4-bf17-453242a8afd3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ee86f9fe-677e-40e1-95a3-faddcb0045fc",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let’s look at an example of a statistic.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ee86f9fe-677e-40e1-95a3-faddcb0045fc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7689fe1c-843f-42e4-bf17-453242a8afd3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "96db39f7-2033-4792-a36e-935c4e0607d0",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *mean*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that the mean is a helpful way to get a general sense for a dataset.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk students through the first step of calculating the mean: adding up the data points.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk students through the second step of calculating the mean: dividing by the number of data points.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to calculate their group’s mean guess in their Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *range*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that the range is a helpful way to get a sense for how spread out a dataset is.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk students through computing the range: identifying the largest and smallest values in a dataset.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk students through computing the range: subtracting the smallest value from the largest in the dataset.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to calculate the range of their group’s guesses in their Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Build suspense for the reveal of the number of jelly beans.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal the number of jelly beans in the jar.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have each group share their calculated mean. Pick a winning group—the group who was closest to the actual number of jelly beans.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have each group share their calculated range. Pick a winning group—the one with the smallest range.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "As students share their answers, look for a group with a large range but a mean close to the true answer. If one exists, you can explicitly mention them in the upcoming discussion question. If not, hypothetical values can be used instead."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "96db39f7-2033-4792-a36e-935c4e0607d0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7689fe1c-843f-42e4-bf17-453242a8afd3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d138da36-209f-4258-a3e8-60785d12dc9e",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Consider a group with a mean very close to the answer, but with a wide range. Could we say the members of the group are individually good at guessing? Could we say the group as a whole is good at guessing?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students practice interpreting the mean and range values of data. A strong answer will say that the group might be good at guessing but the individuals might not be. Weaker responses will only make a claim without providing reasoning, or will confuse the interpretations of mean and range. If students are struggling, you may encourage them to try and compare how good an individual in the group is at guessing to how good the group as a whole is, illustrating that the range gives you information about the former, while the mean gives you information about the latter.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If there’s a group that matches this description, consider gauging their reactions to this question."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d138da36-209f-4258-a3e8-60785d12dc9e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7689fe1c-843f-42e4-bf17-453242a8afd3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "76f0a42f-d52c-4089-9f9b-cf44ffc32aca",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Have students compute mean and range programmatically and interpret those values to compare two datasets.",
        "duration": "25",
        "name": "Data Analysis in Python"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "76f0a42f-d52c-4089-9f9b-cf44ffc32aca",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7689fe1c-843f-42e4-bf17-453242a8afd3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9df74310-94ef-43c3-9569-be54f7bb11c6",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Python has a `statistics` module that makes it easier for us to calculate values like mean in our datasets.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9df74310-94ef-43c3-9569-be54f7bb11c6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7689fe1c-843f-42e4-bf17-453242a8afd3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5ef858f3-26db-4575-bd02-0db0c32ea35c",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to the `mean` function, which computes the mean of a list of numbers."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5ef858f3-26db-4575-bd02-0db0c32ea35c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7689fe1c-843f-42e4-bf17-453242a8afd3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "63141853-6dee-4ea1-8cc6-c67bce40549f",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The numbers in this dataset aren’t whole numbers: they have decimal points. Until now, all of the data you’ve been working with has been whole numbers, which Python calls ints. Today, you will be working with decimal numbers. Python calls these floats.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "63141853-6dee-4ea1-8cc6-c67bce40549f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7689fe1c-843f-42e4-bf17-453242a8afd3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "915d23ef-3e50-4ccf-9a1c-4d09d69402d0",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *float*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "915d23ef-3e50-4ccf-9a1c-4d09d69402d0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7689fe1c-843f-42e4-bf17-453242a8afd3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "06c37237-f8f5-4ccc-a391-202ca3141efe",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In computer science, a \"float\" refers to a number with a decimal point. In Python, you can convert a string to float using the built-in float function.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "06c37237-f8f5-4ccc-a391-202ca3141efe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7689fe1c-843f-42e4-bf17-453242a8afd3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3b3d57cc-04ad-472e-9068-60db585ecbb0",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *standard deviation*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3b3d57cc-04ad-472e-9068-60db585ecbb0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7689fe1c-843f-42e4-bf17-453242a8afd3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2836d2b9-7027-4bb6-9da3-4847c11c8932",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Standard deviation is similar to range: they both tell you about how spread out the data is.  The range tells you the biggest difference in the data (it’s the smallest value subtracted from the largest value), while the standard deviation tells you how spread out the data is on average, by comparing each data point to the mean.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2836d2b9-7027-4bb6-9da3-4847c11c8932",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7689fe1c-843f-42e4-bf17-453242a8afd3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "568cb27d-1c29-4e37-8788-98814ef9cda7",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The formula for standard deviation is quite complicated, but luckily, we don’t have to manually compute it when we use Python because the statistics module has a function that can do that calculation for us!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "568cb27d-1c29-4e37-8788-98814ef9cda7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7689fe1c-843f-42e4-bf17-453242a8afd3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9df1ab33-65c2-4d3f-a07a-8083ef3587cd",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Distinguish between range and standard deviation."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9df1ab33-65c2-4d3f-a07a-8083ef3587cd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7689fe1c-843f-42e4-bf17-453242a8afd3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3a69b424-a74c-43a0-8e15-8c2c56316c5e",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "If you’re trying to find out the biggest spread in a dataset, you would look at the range. But if you want to know the average spread, you would calculate the standard deviation.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3a69b424-a74c-43a0-8e15-8c2c56316c5e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7689fe1c-843f-42e4-bf17-453242a8afd3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a58fd544-2a6e-43d6-b0ca-200fb13591d3",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to the `stdev` function, which computes the standard deviation of a list of numbers."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a58fd544-2a6e-43d6-b0ca-200fb13591d3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7689fe1c-843f-42e4-bf17-453242a8afd3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "077b2c53-653f-43ac-98a7-09c2ca845af3",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now, you’ll practice analyzing numerical data using Python.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "077b2c53-653f-43ac-98a7-09c2ca845af3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7689fe1c-843f-42e4-bf17-453242a8afd3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c35e6140-674e-49a7-aa72-c340c84ef52f",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Data Analysis with Python"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c35e6140-674e-49a7-aa72-c340c84ef52f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7689fe1c-843f-42e4-bf17-453242a8afd3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "31b22336-3df0-4b89-9051-3fb2175160d5",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Levels 1–4.\n- **Level 1:** Students predict what code that reads two CSVs will do, learning about append.\n- **Level 2:** Students cast strings from their CSVs to floats.\n- **Level 3:** Students calculate the mean and standard deviation of two datasets using the statistics module.\n- **Level 4:** Students complete a Check Your Understanding.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 4 as an opportunity to assess students’ ability to interpret mean, range, and standard deviation correctly. In the scenarios that students are working with, arguments for either dataset are plausible depending on the justification."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "31b22336-3df0-4b89-9051-3fb2175160d5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7689fe1c-843f-42e4-bf17-453242a8afd3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "24d3715e-4c1b-4ae9-9f91-d9b8e2e90674",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "24d3715e-4c1b-4ae9-9f91-d9b8e2e90674",
        "lesson_activity.key": "245ee512-b638-4bb9-a942-dcf20946fd48"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "60b7dba2-c059-40f0-b04d-7c8109112b7c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Imagine a musician who is interested in analyzing data about sales of their albums.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "60b7dba2-c059-40f0-b04d-7c8109112b7c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "068d9d43-31bb-4630-a10f-13d948decdb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "46085198-674c-43a8-ad9e-b3603cde3f67",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Look at the columns of data in the musician’s dataset. What questions could they answer based on the data?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify questions that might be answerable based on data by studying the columns of a dataset, without yet looking at any of the particulars of the data. This prepares students to identify and investigate questions in their own project. Strong responses will look at specific columns of data and identify questions that relate to them: for example, students might ask what the average number of streams on an album is, or what the best-selling album was. Particularly strong responses will look at relationships between variables in the dataset: for example, students might ask what music genres get the most streams, or whether the budget affects how many albums are sold. If students struggle to identify quantitative questions, pick a column or two from the dataset and prompt students to think about why they might be useful to know about.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** As students come up with possible questions, write on the board some patterns from different types of questions that are surfaced. For example, questions might begin with “How many…”, “What’s the average…”, “How consistent is…”, “What’s the best…”, “What percent of…”. Students can use these patterns later to propose their own questions about their dataset.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "46085198-674c-43a8-ad9e-b3603cde3f67",
        "lesson_activity.key": "068d9d43-31bb-4630-a10f-13d948decdb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "15c631cd-57b8-43ac-870a-ef09d3e3cb05",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In this project, you’ll be asking your own questions about a dataset and using the data to find answers.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "15c631cd-57b8-43ac-870a-ef09d3e3cb05",
        "lesson_activity.key": "068d9d43-31bb-4630-a10f-13d948decdb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "315754f9-e2e0-4122-9a9b-abcfc7b851db",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "315754f9-e2e0-4122-9a9b-abcfc7b851db",
        "lesson_activity.key": "068d9d43-31bb-4630-a10f-13d948decdb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "165dc6e4-7d76-4e57-a001-80d16d02ebf9",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students explore scenarios where data analysis can be helpful, pick a dataset of interest to them, and identify how data could be useful in practice.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Hand out the Analyze a Dataset Project Planning Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the project requirements and project rubric.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Invite students to ask questions about the project.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Choose a Dataset"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "165dc6e4-7d76-4e57-a001-80d16d02ebf9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "068d9d43-31bb-4630-a10f-13d948decdb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cba50298-21a0-4e9d-aca2-c5ef9d519c49",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Lots of different organizations might have data that could be analyzed. In this project, you’ll pick an organization’s data to explore and you’ll answer questions that could help those organizations make decisions.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cba50298-21a0-4e9d-aca2-c5ef9d519c49",
        "lesson_activity.key": "068d9d43-31bb-4630-a10f-13d948decdb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fc92de73-e265-46b2-913a-6aa0a707cb8e",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the five different scenarios that students can choose to investigate in this project.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete Step 1 of their Project Planning Guide. Students will choose one of five datasets to explore and consider how the data could be useful to an organization.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Help students explore the properties in the dataset and pick one to focus on. If students struggle to answer the question about data could be useful, encourage them to think about why an organization might be interested in collecting data in the first place. In addition, you can also brainstorm with students to think about what kinds of decisions an organization might need to make and to consider how data might be helpful to inform those decisions.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fc92de73-e265-46b2-913a-6aa0a707cb8e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "068d9d43-31bb-4630-a10f-13d948decdb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "efdf2775-8b9c-4c3b-9ad4-5606fdec9ec3",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students explore a dataset, pose specific questions that can be answered by data, and begin writing Python code to conduct data analysis.",
        "duration": "25",
        "name": "Begin Data Investigation"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "efdf2775-8b9c-4c3b-9ad4-5606fdec9ec3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "068d9d43-31bb-4630-a10f-13d948decdb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8a842ff1-674d-4208-b368-039add467a9a",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Investigating Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8a842ff1-674d-4208-b368-039add467a9a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "068d9d43-31bb-4630-a10f-13d948decdb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1d05c314-1ce5-4d1c-be51-529e59692c84",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Levels 1-2.\n- Level 1: Students explore a dataset and write down questions they want to answer from the data.\n- Level 2: Students start writing Python code to analyze the data and print the results of their analysis. It’s okay if students don’t finish their analysis today: they can continue the project on Day 2 of the lesson.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students begin their data analysis, help them brainstorm questions and write Python code to answer them.\n- Some students have trouble coming up with relevant questions. You can point them towards the question patterns that were surfaced in the warm up as a starting point for writing questions. You can also encourage students to look at particular columns of data in the spreadsheet and consider what information might be contained in the data for that column. For students who are feeling more comfortable, encourage them to think about the relationship between data in different columns.\n- Students might struggle with the open-endedness of the Python programming in Level 2. Students are given some starter code that opens a CSV file, but students are responsible for determining what programming constructs they need to answer the questions that they are interested in. Help students come up with the programming constructs they might need to answer questions: for example, students will likely need to write a loop that iterates over the CSV file. Students will likely need to use dictionary syntax to access particular values in each row. Students might need to cast values to int and float. Students might also use functions like min, max, or statistics module functions to analyze data too.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1d05c314-1ce5-4d1c-be51-529e59692c84",
        "lesson_activity.key": "068d9d43-31bb-4630-a10f-13d948decdb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6c092b95-044b-4149-963f-240ca5e940ff",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Day 1 Reflection section in their Project Planning Guide.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6c092b95-044b-4149-963f-240ca5e940ff",
        "lesson_activity.key": "068d9d43-31bb-4630-a10f-13d948decdb6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e9beab65-1739-4ce1-9eb9-71a59ca1ddd2",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students into pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to share their project progress with each other. Give students an opportunity to surface areas where they’ve encountered challenges in conducting their data analysis, and encourage students to give each other suggestions.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e9beab65-1739-4ce1-9eb9-71a59ca1ddd2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "df06d61e-5f3e-41e5-b5ae-71ff5318f042"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8e18d439-f694-4795-bf1d-19433bfefa4c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students write Python code to investigate questions of their choosing. Then, students look at the output of their program and interpret the results to answer their questions.",
        "duration": "25",
        "name": "Conduct Data Analysis"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8e18d439-f694-4795-bf1d-19433bfefa4c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "df06d61e-5f3e-41e5-b5ae-71ff5318f042"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "694d1b7b-e7c1-48bc-b40c-4a36f6da358e",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Interpreting Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "694d1b7b-e7c1-48bc-b40c-4a36f6da358e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "df06d61e-5f3e-41e5-b5ae-71ff5318f042"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4ac5d8d4-c1ef-48b1-9a0a-e4ac04eca7aa",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Levels 2-3.\n- Level 2: Students continue writing Python code to analyze their dataset.\n- Level 3: Students interpret the results of their Python program and write about the answers to their questions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Help students analyze data and interpret the results of their analysis.\n- If students are struggling with writing a program to answer all of their questions, encourage them to split the program into parts and work on one question at a time.\n- Help students come up with the programming constructs they might need to answer questions: for example, students will likely need to write a loop that iterates over the CSV file. Students will likely need to use dictionary syntax to access particular values in each row. Students might need to cast values to int and float. Students might also use functions like min, max, or statistics module functions to analyze data too.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4ac5d8d4-c1ef-48b1-9a0a-e4ac04eca7aa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "df06d61e-5f3e-41e5-b5ae-71ff5318f042"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "86f2c7f4-925f-4024-b4fc-1ab836cc0d16",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students have an opportunity to share their findings from data analysis with others.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Help students form small groups.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students share the work they did in their project. Each student can share the dataset they chose, the questions they asked, and what they learned from their data analysis.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Share Data Analysis"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "86f2c7f4-925f-4024-b4fc-1ab836cc0d16",
        "lesson_activity.key": "df06d61e-5f3e-41e5-b5ae-71ff5318f042"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "910f5983-f57d-489f-bbbb-1f72b81ab491",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "910f5983-f57d-489f-bbbb-1f72b81ab491",
        "lesson_activity.key": "df06d61e-5f3e-41e5-b5ae-71ff5318f042"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "22f6a00c-d9fb-41d9-b3a1-31bc2990eca7",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Before students enter, leave copies of the American Time Use Survey handout—containing raw results from the survey—in places where groups of students can look at it together.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** To begin class, prompt groups of students to read the handout and see what interesting things they can find. Give them about 3–4 minutes to do so. The goal isn’t that students find insights quickly: they may find it difficult to read the data, and that’s okay. In fact, the goal is for students to experience contrast: between how comparatively difficult it is to sift through raw data, and how comparatively easy it is to find interesting things in a good visualization.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** After about 3–4 minutes, prompt a few groups to share what they’ve found."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "22f6a00c-d9fb-41d9-b3a1-31bc2990eca7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a74e16e5-7544-41f1-aace-f3aa41ffaaa5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a7744851-b5ca-487d-951c-bdf2814ef618",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This data by itself can be difficult to read. Let’s make things a little easier for ourselves with a visualization.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a7744851-b5ca-487d-951c-bdf2814ef618",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a74e16e5-7544-41f1-aace-f3aa41ffaaa5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6c9c3bfd-4dd2-42b6-983a-1142437485b1",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce a visualization of the data from the American Time Use Survey. Point out how to read the chart: each column represents one hour in the day. Each section in the column represents the proportion of Americans engaging in the activity listed in the legend.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to call out interesting things they notice from the visualization. Consider scaffolding their insights with questions such as \"When does school seem to start?\", \"When are most people asleep?\", or \"When are people most likely to be doing something outside of work?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What does this visualization make it easier to see? How so?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to reflect on what the visualization adds to their understanding, as well as on how it does so. Strong responses will connect a specific element of the visualization (e.g., \"the stacked and colored bars\") to a particular insight (e.g., \"they make it easier to see how much time people spend doing one thing vs. another\"). If students struggle to identify features of the chart that are helpful, consider pointing out visual features and asking students what they help make clearer. Once students have pointed out a few helpful features of the chart, move on."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6c9c3bfd-4dd2-42b6-983a-1142437485b1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a74e16e5-7544-41f1-aace-f3aa41ffaaa5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2e937ad2-faa4-4a6e-ad95-ff650f1086ec",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today we’ll start an exploration of how to visualize data. Visualization is a way to see things about data we might otherwise miss.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2e937ad2-faa4-4a6e-ad95-ff650f1086ec",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a74e16e5-7544-41f1-aace-f3aa41ffaaa5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bbc18c7d-9101-44aa-8afe-9ac0770ffe53",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bbc18c7d-9101-44aa-8afe-9ac0770ffe53",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a74e16e5-7544-41f1-aace-f3aa41ffaaa5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5e233eb2-5b25-411c-90ca-073484aad8cd",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce students to libraries and practice visualizing numerical data with `matplotlib`.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to recall the scatter plots they saw in Unit 2.",
        "duration": "12",
        "name": "Visualizing Numerical Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5e233eb2-5b25-411c-90ca-073484aad8cd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "54b2085a-61ad-45cb-be3e-e133d58c9d96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "972cb690-dc40-4aec-aa0a-a3b4da3786ce",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Remember that, in Unit 2 and beyond, we worked with scatter plots. These helped us visualize the relationship between two numerical variables—like an artist’s number of monthly listeners on Spotify and their number of social media followers. Today, you’re learning how to make one yourself.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "972cb690-dc40-4aec-aa0a-a3b4da3786ce",
        "lesson_activity.key": "54b2085a-61ad-45cb-be3e-e133d58c9d96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7a81f9c6-ac81-482c-9a6e-eb91f0a3a847",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Visualizing Numerical Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7a81f9c6-ac81-482c-9a6e-eb91f0a3a847",
        "lesson_activity.key": "54b2085a-61ad-45cb-be3e-e133d58c9d96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a75fa2c7-ae4f-49b2-89fa-be081a847f62",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 1.\n- **Level 1:** Students read a simple Python program that uses `matplotlib` to create a scatter plot. They predict what it will do, then run the program to check their prediction.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students read the provided code, offer hints and guidance to students who seem stuck. Alternatively, if some students have finished much more quickly than others, consider inviting them to help others interpret the code.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *library*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a75fa2c7-ae4f-49b2-89fa-be081a847f62",
        "lesson_activity.key": "54b2085a-61ad-45cb-be3e-e133d58c9d96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e923c524-04d9-4216-bee1-9dc5ba2b220a",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The program you just read uses a library called `matplotlib`. This is a common library data analysts use to build visualizations.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e923c524-04d9-4216-bee1-9dc5ba2b220a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "54b2085a-61ad-45cb-be3e-e133d58c9d96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "53c84f7b-5b0d-4b76-aac4-2b3351ce191d",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to annotate the starter code in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the starter code line by line, describing what each part does."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "53c84f7b-5b0d-4b76-aac4-2b3351ce191d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "54b2085a-61ad-45cb-be3e-e133d58c9d96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f9631e84-4c6c-4f1d-9391-910e62ec1c91",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "First, your starter code imports a module called `matplotlib.pyplot`. This is a module with functions, like scatter, to create plots. `matplotlib.pyplot` is a lot to type, so we can call it something shorter with `as`. The keyword `as` lets you refer to a module as another, often shorter, name.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f9631e84-4c6c-4f1d-9391-910e62ec1c91",
        "lesson_activity.key": "54b2085a-61ad-45cb-be3e-e133d58c9d96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "50816f4a-9a37-444f-bfca-83324c49d44c",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "`plt.scatter` takes two lists. The first one, followers, goes on the horizontal axis. The second, listeners, goes on the vertical axis.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "50816f4a-9a37-444f-bfca-83324c49d44c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "54b2085a-61ad-45cb-be3e-e133d58c9d96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8a01b835-15e8-40fc-a121-1e94591ea1f4",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "`plt.show`, at the bottom of the starter code, is what reveals your plot. Without it, your plot won’t be shown! It should be the last function you call in your program.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8a01b835-15e8-40fc-a121-1e94591ea1f4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "54b2085a-61ad-45cb-be3e-e133d58c9d96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "93b6f512-df62-486f-a920-e0daf56f4b2c",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Visualizing Numerical Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "93b6f512-df62-486f-a920-e0daf56f4b2c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "54b2085a-61ad-45cb-be3e-e133d58c9d96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "edea2648-462c-41ae-bcc3-6b64f6dac3bc",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 2.\n- **Level 2:** Students modify the program in Level 1, plotting additional data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students update the scatter plot from Level 1, look for…\n- Students who are unsure where to add the new data. Point them to the lists of current data, then remind them how to add data to a list.\n- Students who find the exercise easy and want to move on quickly. Prompt them to see what happens when they add data that’s a far outlier from their current data points: how does that change their plot?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "edea2648-462c-41ae-bcc3-6b64f6dac3bc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "54b2085a-61ad-45cb-be3e-e133d58c9d96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "50e49f34-9014-44e0-a36a-2e4c7dca1e12",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Practice visualizing categorical data with `matplotlib`.",
        "duration": "8",
        "name": "Visualizing Categorical Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "50e49f34-9014-44e0-a36a-2e4c7dca1e12",
        "lesson_activity.key": "54b2085a-61ad-45cb-be3e-e133d58c9d96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c9d9fc46-10d4-4d84-847f-a1243170ff88",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You’ve learned how to visualize numerical data with matplotlib. Now, let’s learn how to visualize other kinds of data.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c9d9fc46-10d4-4d84-847f-a1243170ff88",
        "lesson_activity.key": "54b2085a-61ad-45cb-be3e-e133d58c9d96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fa317c01-9eb2-4f9f-bb63-98a90cbe89ac",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to recall the bar charts they saw in Unit 2."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fa317c01-9eb2-4f9f-bb63-98a90cbe89ac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "54b2085a-61ad-45cb-be3e-e133d58c9d96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6cc2103a-425b-4ac3-bc8a-91064fffd4a1",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Remember that, in Unit 2 and beyond, we worked with bar charts. These helped us visualize categorical data—like how many people like different genres of music. Today, you’re learning how to make one yourself.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6cc2103a-425b-4ac3-bc8a-91064fffd4a1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "54b2085a-61ad-45cb-be3e-e133d58c9d96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d1d4ea4f-daaf-481b-b009-7b84082233d2",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Visualizing Categorical Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d1d4ea4f-daaf-481b-b009-7b84082233d2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "54b2085a-61ad-45cb-be3e-e133d58c9d96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9b215831-b423-4edb-b5e4-a39b5d227892",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Levels 3–4.\n- **Level 3:** Students read a simple Python program that uses matplotlib to create a bar chart. They predict what it will do, then run the program to check their prediction.\n- **Level 4:** Students modify the program in Level 3, plotting additional data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students learn about bar charts in `matplotlib`, point out that the program in Level 3 is very similar to the program in Level 1. However, instead of using `plt.scatter`, the program in Level 3 uses `plt.bar`. This simple change is an example of the power of a library: it offers simple functions that can quickly accomplish what you want."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9b215831-b423-4edb-b5e4-a39b5d227892",
        "lesson_activity.key": "54b2085a-61ad-45cb-be3e-e133d58c9d96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "69726e15-9b38-4b13-8b94-7a91975e4787",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Practice independently visualizing data of a student’s choice.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Choosing a Visualization"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "69726e15-9b38-4b13-8b94-7a91975e4787",
        "lesson_activity.key": "54b2085a-61ad-45cb-be3e-e133d58c9d96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "09cc5d17-e01e-40dc-be68-67666ae91d2b",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You’ve explored two ways to build plots with `matplotlib`. Next, spend some time building a visualization of your choice.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "09cc5d17-e01e-40dc-be68-67666ae91d2b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "54b2085a-61ad-45cb-be3e-e133d58c9d96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0e6bd251-6004-4408-b386-05532aea1819",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Choosing a Visualization"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0e6bd251-6004-4408-b386-05532aea1819",
        "lesson_activity.key": "54b2085a-61ad-45cb-be3e-e133d58c9d96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8155e4d4-fc89-4fc5-9728-2bf50e010ac6",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Levels 5–6.\n- **Level 5:** Students choose one of two data sets to visualize. Given the data, they must choose whether to use `plt.scatter` or `plt.bar`. They write most of the program themselves.\n- **Level 6:** Students complete a Check Your Understanding about libraries.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work on their visualizations, look for…\n- Students who aren’t sure where to start. Prompt them to look at the code in their Unit Guide as a representative example of the program they will write.\n- Students who choose `plt.scatter` or `plt.bar` incorrectly. Prompt them to consider whether the chart they’re attempting to build is the best fit for their data, and guide them to the realization they should choose a different visualization."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8155e4d4-fc89-4fc5-9728-2bf50e010ac6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "54b2085a-61ad-45cb-be3e-e133d58c9d96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9f54dda8-5f51-4867-939c-a152fd69b32c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *If you had more time, what else would you want to add to your visualizations?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Peek ahead to the following lesson, in which students learn to customize plots in `matplotlib`. There are no wrong answers: just encourage students to quickly share what they might want their visualizations to look like in the future, as they gain more skills."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9f54dda8-5f51-4867-939c-a152fd69b32c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9151b230-58ac-4b64-bfc6-6a916ec8c8e4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4ef38db4-37d6-4fd8-be74-9679e23cd2f2",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Next lesson, we’ll learn to customize the plots we make—by adding labels, adopting color schemes, and adjusting limits.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4ef38db4-37d6-4fd8-be74-9679e23cd2f2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9151b230-58ac-4b64-bfc6-6a916ec8c8e4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3ff523d5-cc32-4fc9-89fd-edcb03878082",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3ff523d5-cc32-4fc9-89fd-edcb03878082",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9151b230-58ac-4b64-bfc6-6a916ec8c8e4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5151b2b3-5da5-46fa-a1ab-fca4ee906252",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which visualization do you think is better? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to surface their intuitions about what makes one visualization better than another. The plot on the right is the correct answer. Strong answers will name particular features of the right-most visualization that make it clearer, more accessible, or more honest than the visualization on the left. Possibilities include:\n- The plot’s labels describe the data.\n- The plot’s legend differentiates between the two groups of data.\n- The different data markers (i.e., stars vs. dots) make the plot easier to read if someone is color-blind.\n- The plot’s limits are more true to the data: the vertical axis is a true 0–10 scale.\n\nIt’s not expected students know the vocabulary to define these features—they’ll learn this vocabulary as the lesson progresses. Try to tie students’ observations to the three goals of data visualizations they’ll learn about in this lesson: clarity, accessibility, and honesty. Once students have identified the relevant features of the better plot, continue on.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Consider noting that these plots use sample data—rather than real data—for illustrative purposes."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5151b2b3-5da5-46fa-a1ab-fca4ee906252",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fc1aac15-a0ea-4aff-b9db-8130a5a35e8d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f92b0b0b-6ed8-4df1-a50e-7113b66267b8",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the three data visualization goals that will structure this lesson.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to write these goals in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f92b0b0b-6ed8-4df1-a50e-7113b66267b8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fc1aac15-a0ea-4aff-b9db-8130a5a35e8d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e83440ce-f113-49e9-9ce4-68b4602e35df",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Prompt students to choose a visualization they’ll improve over the course of the lesson, then introduce the documentation they’ll use to do so.",
        "duration": "6",
        "name": "Documentation"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e83440ce-f113-49e9-9ce4-68b4602e35df",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e738204-a901-4779-8cf7-4acebde62be3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fef533e6-9b54-4a00-9d27-60520cc1ac9f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Your task is to take a basic plot and improve it. First, choose the data you want to visualize.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fef533e6-9b54-4a00-9d27-60520cc1ac9f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e738204-a901-4779-8cf7-4acebde62be3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "19d8a771-01f7-4581-b26f-476ed6a68381",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "A Basic Plot"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "19d8a771-01f7-4581-b26f-476ed6a68381",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e738204-a901-4779-8cf7-4acebde62be3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aee4df98-cf80-47b6-b94f-d5f3facda19d",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 8, Level 1.\n- **Level 1:** Students choose the data they want to visualize. They’re given code that creates a basic plot for the data. Once all students have chosen their data and viewed their starting plot, continue on."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aee4df98-cf80-47b6-b94f-d5f3facda19d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e738204-a901-4779-8cf7-4acebde62be3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0d386545-0066-46c0-9451-0cb21f9e9ad8",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that you’ve chosen your plot, let’s focus on how you’ll improve it.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0d386545-0066-46c0-9451-0cb21f9e9ad8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e738204-a901-4779-8cf7-4acebde62be3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c43603bd-36b7-4a9f-9a57-72e4ae158ebd",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *documentation*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c43603bd-36b7-4a9f-9a57-72e4ae158ebd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e738204-a901-4779-8cf7-4acebde62be3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "89b4a719-d7c3-40d7-bb0e-fb1369ffb9d3",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You learned last time that `matplotlib` is a library—a bundle of code someone else has written. When people write code they want others to use, they often also write documentation: information about how to use their code. To make better charts today, you’ll use a simplified version of the `matplotlib` documentation.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "89b4a719-d7c3-40d7-bb0e-fb1369ffb9d3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e738204-a901-4779-8cf7-4acebde62be3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e9281603-193c-481b-8be0-aa430f8f8c98",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the `matplotlib` Documentation Handout.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point out the main elements of the documentation: the functions students can use, the parameters those functions take, and the example code students can reference. It’s not necessary to introduce each function: students will learn about the functions just in time, as the lesson progresses.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *keyword argument*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e9281603-193c-481b-8be0-aa430f8f8c98",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e738204-a901-4779-8cf7-4acebde62be3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4050e2d2-6e12-48a0-a12c-25ffe45ed998",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Some of the functions in the `matplotlib` documentation use keyword arguments. Here, \"large\" is a keyword argument: it’s matched to the size parameter by name. \"Title\" is not: it’s matched to its parameter by its position in the argument list.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4050e2d2-6e12-48a0-a12c-25ffe45ed998",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e738204-a901-4779-8cf7-4acebde62be3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2950fc8c-1390-4d02-a34d-ec9542e00c7a",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce labels in `matplotlib`, in service of improving a visualization’s clarity.",
        "duration": "8",
        "name": "Labels and Clarity"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2950fc8c-1390-4d02-a34d-ec9542e00c7a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e738204-a901-4779-8cf7-4acebde62be3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d706b2f9-3f50-4de4-9655-a377685a04a1",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "First, let’s focus on improving your visualization’s clarity. One way to improve clarity is to label your visualization.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d706b2f9-3f50-4de4-9655-a377685a04a1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e738204-a901-4779-8cf7-4acebde62be3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e557eb8c-7f09-4508-b95b-86332202ae03",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce, by example, the kinds of labels students will add to their plot: a title, a horizontal axis label, a vertical axis label, and a legend. Point out why these elements improve the plot’s clarity."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e557eb8c-7f09-4508-b95b-86332202ae03",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e738204-a901-4779-8cf7-4acebde62be3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a32dd5ba-70f7-46e6-a272-925c784ee9c2",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Labels and Clarity"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a32dd5ba-70f7-46e6-a272-925c784ee9c2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e738204-a901-4779-8cf7-4acebde62be3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9aacd761-6201-4e92-b4f2-e71f64ed21f4",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 8, Level 2.\n- **Level 2:** Students reference the `matplotlib` documentation to add a title, a horizontal axis label, a vertical axis label, and a legend to their plot."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9aacd761-6201-4e92-b4f2-e71f64ed21f4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e738204-a901-4779-8cf7-4acebde62be3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c470a7a6-fec1-404b-9125-03090091934d",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As you add labels to your plot, make sure they come before `plt.show`. That’s the last function your program should call, only after you’ve added your labels. Similarly, make sure you use `plt.legend` after `plt.scatter`.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students add labels to their plots, look for…\n- Students who use the labeling functions after plt.show. The plt.show function must be the last function called in their program.\n- Students who use plt.legend before plt.scatter in their code. plt.legend must come after plt.scatter for the legend to appear.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c470a7a6-fec1-404b-9125-03090091934d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e738204-a901-4779-8cf7-4acebde62be3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "095818ef-b52d-45e9-aed6-388b7bffd536",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce colors and markers in `matplotlib`, in service of improving a visualization’s accessibility.",
        "duration": "8",
        "name": "Style and Accessibility"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "095818ef-b52d-45e9-aed6-388b7bffd536",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e738204-a901-4779-8cf7-4acebde62be3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "601bfa4b-14c7-49e2-a7c4-3411d9db59e2",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now, let’s focus on improving your visualization’s accessibility. One way to improve accessibility is to ensure your chart uses a colorblind-friendly palette: for example, one light color and one dark color are easier to tell apart than two colors that are similar to each other. Or, you could make sure your chart doesn’t rely entirely on color to communicate information.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "601bfa4b-14c7-49e2-a7c4-3411d9db59e2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e738204-a901-4779-8cf7-4acebde62be3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9c298774-4b97-482f-86b7-5cddda8bfb1e",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce, by example, the kinds of styles students can update: they can change the color and marker of the plotted data points."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9c298774-4b97-482f-86b7-5cddda8bfb1e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e738204-a901-4779-8cf7-4acebde62be3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d94732bb-136d-4e58-a9bf-cf5b99b31f7a",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Style and Accessibility"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d94732bb-136d-4e58-a9bf-cf5b99b31f7a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e738204-a901-4779-8cf7-4acebde62be3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b0a61708-64b9-4bbf-899a-ffbb2bc0734e",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 8, Level 3.\n- **Level 3:** Students reference the `matplotlib` documentation to update their data’s color and markers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students add update their plots, look for…\n- Students who aren’t using keyword arguments for color and marker on plt.scatter. Per the example in the documentation, color and marker are keyword arguments."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b0a61708-64b9-4bbf-899a-ffbb2bc0734e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e738204-a901-4779-8cf7-4acebde62be3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a5c0a37c-8880-4a1a-bdc5-12f3dcd3f8d3",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce limits in `matplotlib`, in service of improving a visualization’s honesty.",
        "duration": "8",
        "name": "Limits and Honesty"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a5c0a37c-8880-4a1a-bdc5-12f3dcd3f8d3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e738204-a901-4779-8cf7-4acebde62be3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a9611b79-7d97-47f4-bb27-0c8a6a564e1f",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Finally, let’s focus on improving your visualization’s honesty. One way to improve honesty is to ensure your plot uses appropriate limits.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a9611b79-7d97-47f4-bb27-0c8a6a564e1f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e738204-a901-4779-8cf7-4acebde62be3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "071412dd-5f0a-46b6-92fc-6227daf6300f",
      "position": 23,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce, by example, what’s meant by a plot’s \"limits\": the values where the vertical and horizontal axes start and end."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "071412dd-5f0a-46b6-92fc-6227daf6300f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e738204-a901-4779-8cf7-4acebde62be3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5c6e6495-e1a9-4546-a9b3-9206c790dc61",
      "position": 24,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Limits and Honesty"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5c6e6495-e1a9-4546-a9b3-9206c790dc61",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e738204-a901-4779-8cf7-4acebde62be3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1121f0b2-8c17-403b-b9a7-d2211dbd7991",
      "position": 25,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 8, Levels 4–5.\n- **Level 4:** Students reference the `matplotlib` documentation to update their plot’s limits.\n- **Level 5:** Students complete a Check Your Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students add update their plots, look for…\n- Students who choose limits outside of the data’s range. They won’t be able to see their data when it’s plotted. Remind students that the first number indicates the lowest value on a given axis, and the second number indicates the highest value on a given axis.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "To set their plot’s limits, students will need to use a tuple of numbers. Optionally define a tuple as being similar to a list, but of a fixed length."
          },
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If there’s additional time after students finish, consider inviting students to present the plots they’ve made. Ask them why they chose the labels, colors, markers, and limits that they did."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 5 to assess whether students have mapped the customizations they’ve learned—labels, colors, markers, and limits—to the goals of plots—clarity, accessibility, and honesty. Look for students to say that a clear plot is one is labeled, an accessible plot is one that has alternative forms of marking, and an honest plot is one that presents the data in a form that’s true to its nature."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1121f0b2-8c17-403b-b9a7-d2211dbd7991",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9e738204-a901-4779-8cf7-4acebde62be3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b6d65a4d-736b-40b6-b68e-54f655622a98",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b6d65a4d-736b-40b6-b68e-54f655622a98",
        "lesson_activity.key": "307b6284-1554-45c0-9db3-aaf231b08526"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "939c735e-8438-424b-a1e8-16ee52a351f4",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Spotlight the role of a data journalist. Explain that data journalists collect, analyze, and present data to tell stories and report news. Data journalists use their ability to work with data to help tell stories and report news.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the “What is data journalism?” video (0:00-1:30), which lets students hear from real data journalists talking about their roles and the work they do."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "939c735e-8438-424b-a1e8-16ee52a351f4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4ae7520-0bfb-46db-a300-062e2c98014a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "655243df-9c8d-4086-8631-f8463b5e367c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Data journalists look at data and communicate about it. Here’s an example: a school collects data on the number of students in clubs each year.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "655243df-9c8d-4086-8631-f8463b5e367c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4ae7520-0bfb-46db-a300-062e2c98014a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ef0a706e-ce36-4322-9635-224766d1a548",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which of these headlines tells a more accurate story?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students look at some data and evaluate how that data could be used to tell a story. Strong responses will identify that each headline has a factual basis: the birdwatching club did triple in size, and the robotics club did add 20 students. Responses might also identify ways that a headline might be misleading: for example, the birdwatching headline might suggest that the total number of students in the club is larger than it actually is. Help students identify that both headlines might factually be true, but they tell different stories based on how each one presents the same raw data. If students struggle to evaluate the headlines, invite them to interpret the data themselves: how would they write a story describing significant information contained in the table?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ef0a706e-ce36-4322-9635-224766d1a548",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4ae7520-0bfb-46db-a300-062e2c98014a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "61a01123-4f68-4d18-9393-329e411d9980",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Data journalists need to take information and decide how to communicate it as a story. And when you’re reading a story, it’s often helpful to think about what the underlying data might be. Today, you will get practice with both reading and writing data stories.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "61a01123-4f68-4d18-9393-329e411d9980",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4ae7520-0bfb-46db-a300-062e2c98014a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "334fbe88-f073-455d-9b65-e5bab8cb3015",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "334fbe88-f073-455d-9b65-e5bab8cb3015",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4ae7520-0bfb-46db-a300-062e2c98014a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cea05b5b-7006-4189-94d9-48fa44826eab",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students look at raw data and corresponding data visualizations and headlines. Students practice identifying what kind of story a visualization is telling, and look for ways in which a visualization might be misleading or not completely capture the story told by the data.\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Evaluate the Data Story"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cea05b5b-7006-4189-94d9-48fa44826eab",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c012479e-3398-4eb5-b2c1-1ee31f274fd7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "168332b8-7e8d-4cde-becc-6ecb3abcb4f2",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In a moment, you’ll see a few different data stories. Each story has a dataset, and a visualization that includes a graphic and a headline. For each visualization, you’re responsible for identifying what story the visualization tells and checking it against the original data.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "168332b8-7e8d-4cde-becc-6ecb3abcb4f2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c012479e-3398-4eb5-b2c1-1ee31f274fd7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "67b3a037-e2c2-4d20-a647-d8ef0efc1bcd",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students into pairs. Give each pair a copy of the Data Journalism Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the three exercises in the “Evaluate the Data Story” activity with their partner.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the activity guide, help them compare the story told by a visualization and the story present in data.\n- Some students might simply repeat the visualization title as the “story” told by the visualization. Encourage these students to think about what each headline is trying to say about the world. For example, a headline that makes a claim about surveyed students might be trying to tell a story about students in general; or a headline that states a value has “skyrocketed” might be trying to tell a story about longer-term trends.\n- Some students might struggle to identify ways in which a visualization might be misleading. Help draw their attention to parts of the data that might be important for how it is interpreted. For example, students might pay attention to the axes on a graph to see what values they span. Students might also notice both the absolute and relative sizes of changes in different values in a dataset.\n- Encourage students to collaborate with their partner. Students should discuss together what each visualization is trying to say and have a conversation about what choices each visualization makes that contribute to how the story is communicated.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What makes for a good data story? When can a data story be misleading?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on the analysis activity they’ve just completed and think about what makes for effective communication. Drawing on their experience from the activity, students should identify characteristics that make for a good data story. Strong responses might identify that a good data story puts information in context: it considers not just the numbers on their own but what’s relevant about them. Students might also say that a good data story doesn’t exaggerate differences that aren’t significant: just because one number is larger than another doesn’t mean that the difference is notable enough to draw a conclusion. Students might also bring up characteristics of good data stories that weren’t explored in the activity: for example, students might say that a good data story makes clear where the data came from. If students have trouble identifying characteristics of good data stories, make the question more concrete by asking them to think about how they would have revised the visualizations from the previous activity. Their suggestions on how to revise those visualizations can then help lead to conversation about general characteristics of good data journalism. Write some of these characteristics on the board so students can refer to them in the next activity.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "67b3a037-e2c2-4d20-a647-d8ef0efc1bcd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c012479e-3398-4eb5-b2c1-1ee31f274fd7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "529c6141-0aa5-40b3-9b11-9ae2eaee6d3f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students act as data journalists themselves, looking at data, deciding what story the data tells, creating data visualizations, and writing headlines about the data.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Tell the Data Story"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "529c6141-0aa5-40b3-9b11-9ae2eaee6d3f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c012479e-3398-4eb5-b2c1-1ee31f274fd7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b65acfda-4fd1-4c20-b1eb-0da300d144f0",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now, you’ll have a chance to act as a data journalist yourself. With your partner, you’ll look at some data and design visualizations and headlines that tell the story you think is shown by the data.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b65acfda-4fd1-4c20-b1eb-0da300d144f0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c012479e-3398-4eb5-b2c1-1ee31f274fd7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "79bd1110-38e5-42b9-b71b-07877c88738a",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the two exercises in the “Tell the Data Story” activity with their partner.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the activity guide, help them tell stories with data.\n- If students aren’t sure where to start, have them speak aloud the data in each chart and talk about what they notice. Students will likely notice differences between numerical values, at which point you can encourage them to think about the relative size of that difference and what it might mean in the context of what the data is measuring.\n- Students are welcome to choose whatever kinds of visualizations they think best communicates each story. Students might choose to use bar charts, stacked bar charts, line graphs, pie charts, or other infographics. Students don’t necessarily need to represent every number from the dataset in their visualization, but if they choose to selectively show only some of the values, talk with students to make sure they’re intentional and thoughtful about how to choose which numbers to represent visually.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If you’re short on time, consider asking students to just choose one of the two stories to create visualizations for. Alternatively, each partner in the pair can pick one of the two stories, and students can share their work with each other after.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "79bd1110-38e5-42b9-b71b-07877c88738a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c012479e-3398-4eb5-b2c1-1ee31f274fd7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5b2bae88-1d54-4f1b-ab70-89990b542fb8",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5b2bae88-1d54-4f1b-ab70-89990b542fb8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c012479e-3398-4eb5-b2c1-1ee31f274fd7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ee21c954-374e-4b14-819a-5a0b682c1f97",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 1.\n- Level 1: Students answer a reflection question based on the visualizations and headlines they wrote.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 1 as a chance to evaluate whether students have identified strategies for communicating with data. Strong responses will consider what’s relevant or important in the data and discuss how that was communicated through visuals and through text. In the apps dataset, for example, there are two apps with a large number of users, so a story that only emphasizes one of them is likely missing part of what’s important in the data. If students are having trouble communicating about data or explaining their approach, consider asking them to compare their own work to the other data stories they explored in the activity guide to see if they’ve avoided the potential issues they identified earlier in the lesson.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ee21c954-374e-4b14-819a-5a0b682c1f97",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c012479e-3398-4eb5-b2c1-1ee31f274fd7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "79ae9a5e-b9e9-44c1-ab0f-ed00ae267d4d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "79ae9a5e-b9e9-44c1-ab0f-ed00ae267d4d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "12a9d73c-b307-4bae-8abc-e0ce8da21e8b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e208b91e-0a58-43ec-a9c2-854e2f5e0fdf",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How do sleep and screen time relate to academic performance? How could we use data to answer this question?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Surface student intuitions around the research question that they will be working with for the lesson. Strong responses will suggest ways that data collection and analysis could help us answer the question, potentially suggesting particular forms of data that would be useful to help answer the question.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e208b91e-0a58-43ec-a9c2-854e2f5e0fdf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e67c919f-5d1a-4cb4-b8c2-beac162469d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cfd8cf42-8efb-4658-bce6-06dca3e86e5d",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, we’ll explore how we could collect data to help us answer this question.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cfd8cf42-8efb-4658-bce6-06dca3e86e5d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e67c919f-5d1a-4cb4-b8c2-beac162469d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ceaa119f-8ad1-4b73-af28-d2042df5d15f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the question of the day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ceaa119f-8ad1-4b73-af28-d2042df5d15f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e67c919f-5d1a-4cb4-b8c2-beac162469d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dd1adc66-76ee-4d18-9fe0-e794bebbcaf9",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce students to sampling strategies and ask them to evaluate the strategies to determine whether they would result in biased or representative data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Remind students about the difference between biased and representative data, and invite students to think about how to collect data in such a way that the collected dataset will be representative.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Exploring Sampling Strategies"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dd1adc66-76ee-4d18-9fe0-e794bebbcaf9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "737191a7-ace3-49f2-a3cc-c4dc09bc21ff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d02c373f-240a-43ca-b357-bea0edd488e3",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "One way to collect representative data of an original group is to collect data that represents the entire group that you’re interested in. For the question that we’re interested in—about sleep, screen time, and academic performance—that could mean collecting data from every student in the school, but that would take a lot of time.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d02c373f-240a-43ca-b357-bea0edd488e3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "737191a7-ace3-49f2-a3cc-c4dc09bc21ff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "32371d9d-1273-461e-adad-da6ff480c494",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _sampling_.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "32371d9d-1273-461e-adad-da6ff480c494",
        "lesson_activity.key": "737191a7-ace3-49f2-a3cc-c4dc09bc21ff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0994142d-3e26-42a0-8864-bc5552d1e8f1",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Rather than collect data from everyone, it’s usually easier to collect data from a sample of people.\n\nWe’re going to explore a few different sampling strategies to collect data for our sleep, screen time, and academic performance research question.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0994142d-3e26-42a0-8864-bc5552d1e8f1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "737191a7-ace3-49f2-a3cc-c4dc09bc21ff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6cd479b8-4863-4045-ae74-ef2a4651b76f",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students a few moments to read the questions that will be asked during sampling.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *You get to school early and ask the first 20 people to arrive the questions. Who might this sampling strategy miss?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that this strategy misses students who arrive late. Strong responses might go further and explain why a student might arrive late: a long bus route, walking from further away, dropping off a younger sibling, or a job. If students are struggling, ask them to imagine the 20 people they'd actually see: “Imagine it’s early in the morning and you’re standing by the entrance to school. What people are you like to see?” Some students might find it easier to first consider who the sampling strategy will include rather than exclude.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students into pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair of student a copy of the Sampling Strategies Activity Guide.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6cd479b8-4863-4045-ae74-ef2a4651b76f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "737191a7-ace3-49f2-a3cc-c4dc09bc21ff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7e790a13-e1ee-454f-8408-a55966b5b1c3",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You will evaluate a few sampling strategies to try and determine whether they would result in biased or representative data.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7e790a13-e1ee-454f-8408-a55966b5b1c3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "737191a7-ace3-49f2-a3cc-c4dc09bc21ff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "885f4a6f-7855-4cd6-ae74-c5879a14d97a",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Invite students to complete the Activity Guide, identifying who is likely to end up in a dataset based on the sampling strategy.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the activity, help them think through which students would be included or missed by each sampling strategy."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "885f4a6f-7855-4cd6-ae74-c5879a14d97a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "737191a7-ace3-49f2-a3cc-c4dc09bc21ff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e1746f2d-2270-41ad-a075-fea6c3588641",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Enable students to explore how to ask questions to collect useful data.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Asking the Right Questions"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e1746f2d-2270-41ad-a075-fea6c3588641",
        "lesson_activity.key": "737191a7-ace3-49f2-a3cc-c4dc09bc21ff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bf547dea-e9d3-41b0-98d5-1e90a44ba435",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Once you have figured out your sampling strategy, you have to decide what questions you will ask the people in your sample. You want your questions to give you useful information, but also to respect people’s privacy.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bf547dea-e9d3-41b0-98d5-1e90a44ba435",
        "lesson_activity.key": "737191a7-ace3-49f2-a3cc-c4dc09bc21ff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c5dbc25c-7adf-4502-9404-daf24a21ccb8",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Do you think students would feel comfortable answering the survey questions? Why or why not?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that some questions feel more sensitive than others, and that this matters when designing a study. Expect GPA to come up as the most uncomfortable — it's a single number that can feel like a judgment about you. Strong responses might also notice that discomfort with questions could affect the data: people may skip questions, round in their favor, or decline to participate, so the responses obtained via data collection might be negatively influenced as a result. If students are stuck, ask which of their answers to the three they'd least want read aloud, and investigate why.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c5dbc25c-7adf-4502-9404-daf24a21ccb8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "737191a7-ace3-49f2-a3cc-c4dc09bc21ff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e73381ad-5b10-4bd5-a7da-4299e22979b0",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In the next activity, you’ll explore ways you can revise these questions to help answer your research question in ways that respect students’ privacy.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e73381ad-5b10-4bd5-a7da-4299e22979b0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "737191a7-ace3-49f2-a3cc-c4dc09bc21ff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9b00c5be-c346-4f22-bef4-747bcb07c3fc",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Asking Questions"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9b00c5be-c346-4f22-bef4-747bcb07c3fc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "737191a7-ace3-49f2-a3cc-c4dc09bc21ff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dd5079db-2210-4931-aabf-0870a1a72d00",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Levels 1–2.\n- Level 1: Students engage in a question writing workshop: students work with the AI chat to come up with three or four to determine academic performance and sleep time or screen time.\n- Level 2: Students complete a Check Your Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, help them with their AI interactions. While they are working on Level 1, help them read and interpret the feedback they’re receiving from AI and encourage them to use that feedback to iterate on their questions.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dd5079db-2210-4931-aabf-0870a1a72d00",
        "lesson_activity.key": "adeab59c-9cce-4dc8-b4da-0aecba8a0194"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f777c538-c282-4227-959c-e376ba158c6c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the key vocabulary.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f777c538-c282-4227-959c-e376ba158c6c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "97bb280e-0ec8-457c-9b52-c062c95e4afa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c66c4aca-9165-42c9-9b35-c5dca1dd082b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce this lesson’s career connection: a Data Scientist."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c66c4aca-9165-42c9-9b35-c5dca1dd082b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2c9e1492-f623-4f69-bb11-8b88a974382a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "487d5910-075d-4e5a-a2a2-a6450e89767d",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today we’re going to take up the work of data scientists. Data scientists clean, prepare, and analyze data to solve real-world problems. Something that frequently surprises people is that data scientists often say most of their time isn’t spent on fancy analysis. Instead, it’s spent getting messy, real-world data into a clean, organized state so they can work with it. That cleaning and organizing work is the skill we’re building today.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "487d5910-075d-4e5a-a2a2-a6450e89767d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2c9e1492-f623-4f69-bb11-8b88a974382a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ff7c4921-6bb6-4a83-9918-530caa6d865c",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, you will be acting as a data scientist regarding an imaginary, fictional expedition. Let’s jump into the story: In 1937, a team of archaeologists called the Kramer Expedition set out to survey a chain of ancient sites. They were never heard from again, and their records were lost. Until now. Their field logbooks have just been recovered, and we have them. Three team members each kept their own logbook. Somewhere in the digitized scans of those pages is the answer to what the expedition discovered. There’s just one problem. Let’s look at one of the recovered pages.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ff7c4921-6bb6-4a83-9918-530caa6d865c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2c9e1492-f623-4f69-bb11-8b88a974382a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "db8adafa-6db4-4e72-99d8-e65f3b2d758a",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here’s a closer look at some of those records.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "db8adafa-6db4-4e72-99d8-e65f3b2d758a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2c9e1492-f623-4f69-bb11-8b88a974382a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e1bb0473-c76b-46e3-8a67-25f0aad26a54",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What’s wrong with these records? What would make them hard to use?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students name concrete problems they can see in the pages. Among possible responses, aim to guide students in seeing…\n- Given two possible logbooks, it isn’t clear which to trust.\n- One of the logbooks is missing data.\n- Sites are given different names (e.g., \"sun temple\" and \"Temple of the Sun\").",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Consider writing the problems students identify on the board, so they can be referenced throughout the lesson."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e1bb0473-c76b-46e3-8a67-25f0aad26a54",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2c9e1492-f623-4f69-bb11-8b88a974382a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "14e87e4e-23cf-4138-9efc-a82e4d5e2018",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the question of the day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "14e87e4e-23cf-4138-9efc-a82e4d5e2018",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2c9e1492-f623-4f69-bb11-8b88a974382a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8b504934-3c07-4efe-a71a-3b990ea06b60",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce students to messy data by posing a question the logbooks, in their current state, can only partly answer: where did the expedition find the most artifacts? Guide students in identifying the traits of the messy data they must fix and in writing a plain-English restoration plan to do so.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Get students into groups of three or four.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Restoring the Archive Activity Guide. Students work in groups of three to four, but each student will complete their own guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give groups about 5 minutes to study the logbooks and attempt the question. Keep this slide up while they work.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for these struggles and nudge rather than resolve:\n- Groups total only one logbook and declare a winner. Ask: “Do the other two logbooks agree with you?”\n- Groups don’t notice that “Temple of the Sun”, “Sun Temple”, and “sun temple” could be the same place. Ask: “How many different sites do you see across all three books?”\n- Groups treat the water-stained entries as zero. Ask: “Does a stain mean nothing was found there?”\n- Groups get stuck converting temperatures. Point out the question only asks about artifacts.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which site did your group pick? What’s your evidence, and what problems did you encounter?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Emphasize the issues with the evidence as given. Strong responses will name a site but recognize the limitations of the data from the Warm Up discussion: some of the log entries are missing, we aren’t sure which log to trust, and sites seem to have slightly different names. Once students have recognized their evidence could use some clean up, continue on.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *data cleaning*.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Restoring the Archive"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8b504934-3c07-4efe-a71a-3b990ea06b60",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9c41b43b-d942-4adc-8fe6-74266bce9cc0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f6772995-498c-42dd-b058-63102b841f96",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The data in the logbooks needs to be cleaned before we can be certain at which site the expedition found the most artifacts.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f6772995-498c-42dd-b058-63102b841f96",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9c41b43b-d942-4adc-8fe6-74266bce9cc0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "640054a3-dc01-47f1-9590-a5a3fb7a221e",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give groups about 5 minutes to complete the Restoration Plan table in the Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for:\n- Rules too vague to code (“fix the names”). Ask: “Which spelling wins? What happens to the others?”\n- Rules that quietly invent data (“guess the stained numbers from nearby rows”). Ask: “Can you trust a number you made up?”\n- Groups that stop at one problem. Point them back to the board list from the warm-up.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask two or three groups to read one rule aloud. Look for moves that relate to the restoration plan students will next implement in Python.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Share an example restoration plan."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "640054a3-dc01-47f1-9590-a5a3fb7a221e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9c41b43b-d942-4adc-8fe6-74266bce9cc0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "125b8830-0119-4f9c-9266-012ac6464759",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here’s an example restoration. It’s not the only correct answer, but it addresses all three problems we’ve been discussing. It’s this restoration plan that you’ll next learn how to implement in Python.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "125b8830-0119-4f9c-9266-012ac6464759",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9c41b43b-d942-4adc-8fe6-74266bce9cc0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "99639e03-dbbe-4860-a9d0-708aab6f8438",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Guide students in translating the restoration plan into Python code.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Restoring the Archive in Code"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "99639e03-dbbe-4860-a9d0-708aab6f8438",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9c41b43b-d942-4adc-8fe6-74266bce9cc0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3e475459-e6c8-4305-85c0-e3a4240ea368",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Restore the Archive"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3e475459-e6c8-4305-85c0-e3a4240ea368",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9c41b43b-d942-4adc-8fe6-74266bce9cc0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2a2589fc-dbc6-40e2-b418-16a00390ee47",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Level 1.\n- **Level 1:** Students combine all three logbooks into a single dataset.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work on Level 1, look for students who aren’t sure where to start. Point out that they can follow the same pattern used to read Logbooks 1 and 2 in the program given to them."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2a2589fc-dbc6-40e2-b418-16a00390ee47",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9c41b43b-d942-4adc-8fe6-74266bce9cc0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "17973d10-29be-4737-bb60-9f602131f193",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Before you continue to Level 2, you’ll need a new Python keyword. Let’s learn that next.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "17973d10-29be-4737-bb60-9f602131f193",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9c41b43b-d942-4adc-8fe6-74266bce9cc0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "24490f19-80c2-4eda-a29e-f5211633f835",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What will this program print?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to predict what the continue keyword will do. Gather a few ideas from students, then reveal the answer.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal what the program will print."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "24490f19-80c2-4eda-a29e-f5211633f835",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9c41b43b-d942-4adc-8fe6-74266bce9cc0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6b753a6d-36d2-4f7e-bc10-05f518fb5373",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This program will only print 0 and 2. Why? Because the continue keyword lets you control how a loop iterates. When you use continue, you tell Python to immediately skip to the next iteration of a loop. This kind of keyword can come in handy when cleaning data.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6b753a6d-36d2-4f7e-bc10-05f518fb5373",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9c41b43b-d942-4adc-8fe6-74266bce9cc0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "46ec90de-bd81-4d3c-99b2-70c7091759b7",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Restore the Archive"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "46ec90de-bd81-4d3c-99b2-70c7091759b7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9c41b43b-d942-4adc-8fe6-74266bce9cc0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7e92eba3-4066-47df-86c9-77d16faa63ed",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Level 2.\n- **Level 2:** Students practice using continue to skip empty data in their dataset.\n- **Level 3:** Students standardize the names of sites to complete the artifact counting program.\n- **Level 4:** Students complete a Check Your Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look for…\n- Students who aren’t sure how to check for missing data. Remind them that they can check if a given string is the empty string by simply comparing their data to the empty string (e.g., data == \"\")\n- Students who miss a name variant (e.g., they’ve fixed \"Causeway\" but not \"broken causeway\"). Encourage them to look carefully at all of the names given to a specific site.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 3 as an assessment opportunity, checking if a student has understood both the three data cleaning strategies—combining files, skipping data, and standardizing data—and how to implement those strategies in Python. Strong programs will be both technically correct and easy to read."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7e92eba3-4066-47df-86c9-77d16faa63ed",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9c41b43b-d942-4adc-8fe6-74266bce9cc0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "047da545-4bd9-4cc0-b671-9224729ff5b8",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Where was the expedition’s discovery? How do you know you can trust that answer?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** The class should converge on Temple of the Sun with 59 artifacts. Good answers will justify this answer by referencing the data cleaning strategies students have just implemented in Python. Even stronger answers may reasonably argue that the data is no more trustworthy than before—students have just made choices about how to clean it up, and those choices may or may not be \"correct.\" Use this discussion to disclaim that data cleaning often happens under uncertainty, and that it’s important to make reasonable decisions while still recognizing limitations."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "047da545-4bd9-4cc0-b671-9224729ff5b8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dcc3053e-54e3-4e04-bf4e-decc69a4a9df"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "80be878a-c82d-4de4-9d10-adc4e786b5a9",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In the next lesson, you’ll start a project where you pose a research question of your own choice and collect your own data to answer it. And here’s a promise: real collected data is always messy. Now you know what to do with it.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "80be878a-c82d-4de4-9d10-adc4e786b5a9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dcc3053e-54e3-4e04-bf4e-decc69a4a9df"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3ccc9add-f389-472b-a6cd-0bca3bcf32d0",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students answer the question of the day in their unit guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the vocabulary from this lesson."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3ccc9add-f389-472b-a6cd-0bca3bcf32d0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dcc3053e-54e3-4e04-bf4e-decc69a4a9df"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a4210085-94a1-4876-8eff-1072c873e8af",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the “Data Analysis Process” as a way to understand the work students have learned to do in this unit.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a4210085-94a1-4876-8eff-1072c873e8af",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00cc85ed-1ebe-46df-ad66-c6194dc3d0d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8809b367-c194-49d4-8576-9b6f0d053fb1",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Through this unit, you’ve learned how to ask questions, how to collect data, how to analyze it, how to visualize it, and how to communicate what you find. Over the next three days, you’re putting all of these parts together.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8809b367-c194-49d4-8576-9b6f0d053fb1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00cc85ed-1ebe-46df-ad66-c6194dc3d0d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "56a1f188-29a0-4dbc-975e-8d88b0b7f62b",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the goal of the Data Research Project."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "56a1f188-29a0-4dbc-975e-8d88b0b7f62b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00cc85ed-1ebe-46df-ad66-c6194dc3d0d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a494f226-3bd6-4074-a6ce-775d30939c7a",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You’ll use what you’ve learned in Unit 3 to answer a question of your own with data analysis.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a494f226-3bd6-4074-a6ce-775d30939c7a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00cc85ed-1ebe-46df-ad66-c6194dc3d0d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "242ba3fc-5ca8-4fe3-aff8-a606c5521c01",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "242ba3fc-5ca8-4fe3-aff8-a606c5521c01",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00cc85ed-1ebe-46df-ad66-c6194dc3d0d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eb10d61e-85c1-4733-a432-039b42efda95",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Guide students in defining their research question and in developing survey questions to collect data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Data Research Project Planning Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the project requirements and project rubric.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Invite students to ask questions about the project.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Asking Questions"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eb10d61e-85c1-4733-a432-039b42efda95",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00cc85ed-1ebe-46df-ad66-c6194dc3d0d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "910e816b-77e2-40a7-9750-0f9b7a487b10",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The first step in the data analysis process is to choose a research question. In this project, your question should ask about the relationship between two numerical variables. Let’s look at a few examples to clarify.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "910e816b-77e2-40a7-9750-0f9b7a487b10",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00cc85ed-1ebe-46df-ad66-c6194dc3d0d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "da438021-2a21-451d-a7c9-01db17863c84",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Share an example research question to spark students’ ideas."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "da438021-2a21-451d-a7c9-01db17863c84",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00cc85ed-1ebe-46df-ad66-c6194dc3d0d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "48d68e13-8a7f-4070-a494-1881806f64a3",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here’s an example question: \"What is the relationship between hours of homework and hours of sleep?\" This question is about two numerical variables: hours of homework, and hours of sleep. They’re numerical because they can be expressed as a number.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "48d68e13-8a7f-4070-a494-1881806f64a3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00cc85ed-1ebe-46df-ad66-c6194dc3d0d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "96c87d94-5510-4179-ac9d-ed361292b9c8",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Share another example research question to spark students’ ideas."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "96c87d94-5510-4179-ac9d-ed361292b9c8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00cc85ed-1ebe-46df-ad66-c6194dc3d0d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3b66081e-4ddf-4d79-8273-7d531f4c4f2c",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here’s another example question: \"What is the relationship between number of sports played and time to run a mile?\" How long it takes someone to run a mile is a duration of time, which could be expressed as a number: for example, 8 minutes.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3b66081e-4ddf-4d79-8273-7d531f4c4f2c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00cc85ed-1ebe-46df-ad66-c6194dc3d0d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "75534043-499f-46c3-a98d-2c403e6aa47e",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Share one more example research question to spark students’ ideas."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "75534043-499f-46c3-a98d-2c403e6aa47e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00cc85ed-1ebe-46df-ad66-c6194dc3d0d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "97eece8c-6a11-49c9-8b03-e9af2f37ee19",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here’s one more example question: \"What is the relationship between liking board games and liking video games?\" At first glance, \"liking something\" doesn’t seem to be a number. But, it might be possible to ask someone to rate how much they like something from 1 to 5, where 1 could mean “Not at all,” 5 could mean “Very much,” and 2–4 could mean varying degrees in the middle.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "In addition to sharing the three example research questions, consider establishing research questions students shouldn’t ask—for privacy or other reasons. For example, it’s likely not appropriate for a student to ask about others’ health information, specific details of home life, or the like."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "97eece8c-6a11-49c9-8b03-e9af2f37ee19",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00cc85ed-1ebe-46df-ad66-c6194dc3d0d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d54b727d-120d-4ca9-aa1e-2fa9253e5fec",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to write a research question in their Project Planning Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students write their research questions, look for questions that don’t meet the requirements. Students’ questions should ask about the relationship between two measurable, numerical variables—which they can learn about from their classmates. If a student’s question is unlikely to be able to be answered, consider nudging them towards a question that could be answered."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d54b727d-120d-4ca9-aa1e-2fa9253e5fec",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00cc85ed-1ebe-46df-ad66-c6194dc3d0d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "48d167a7-08da-4196-b12f-c49894bb1597",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now, it’s time to design survey questions. These questions are what you will use to gather data from your classmates.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "48d167a7-08da-4196-b12f-c49894bb1597",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00cc85ed-1ebe-46df-ad66-c6194dc3d0d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "27b8ea40-bdb6-496c-9e89-769ce6863eb3",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Remind students about a previous example research question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Share example survey questions for the given example research question."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "27b8ea40-bdb6-496c-9e89-769ce6863eb3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00cc85ed-1ebe-46df-ad66-c6194dc3d0d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6dfcaefa-fcb7-49fd-992b-bc1e66c09487",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Notice that each variable—liking board games and liking video games—gets its own question. That way, when you collect data, you have data on both variables.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6dfcaefa-fcb7-49fd-992b-bc1e66c09487",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00cc85ed-1ebe-46df-ad66-c6194dc3d0d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6ee438e0-e9bc-4eec-a7e3-26a2f9afeb0e",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to write two survey questions in their Project Planning Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students write their survey questions, look for…\n- Questions that don’t meet the requirements—per the Project Planning Guide. If a student’s questions do not meet the requirements, point out how they could modify their questions to meet the requirements.\n- Students whose questions are ambiguous. Guide them in making those questions more precise. For example, a question such as \"How many sports do you play?\" might be ambiguous, because what counts as \"playing a sport\" could vary: does one need to do it competitively, or just casually?",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "In addition to sharing the sample survey questions, consider again establishing survey questions that students shouldn’t ask—for privacy or other reasons. For example, it’s likely not appropriate for a student to ask about other students’ health information."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6ee438e0-e9bc-4eec-a7e3-26a2f9afeb0e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00cc85ed-1ebe-46df-ad66-c6194dc3d0d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5c925ac5-d493-492e-a477-f461deb7b947",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Guide students in using their survey questions to collect data from classmates.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct students on how the data collection process will unfold.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Collecting Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5c925ac5-d493-492e-a477-f461deb7b947",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00cc85ed-1ebe-46df-ad66-c6194dc3d0d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9d358545-ce46-411e-9130-96cb0b60af07",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In a moment, you’ll collect data from a group of about six students. First, you’ll write your survey questions on the page of your Activity Guide dedicated to data collection. Then, you’ll pass your own Activity Guide to your left, answer the questions you receive from your neighbor, and continue passing until you receive your Activity Guide again.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9d358545-ce46-411e-9130-96cb0b60af07",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00cc85ed-1ebe-46df-ad66-c6194dc3d0d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a28650b5-3593-4c66-bfb1-f7c3e0d7bd5a",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to sit in groups of about six.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to write their two questions in the appropriate space as they prepare to collect data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Guide students as they collect data from classmates.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** While students are collecting data in their Project Planning Guides, ensure students are following the data collection process efficiently and correctly.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Consider setting a recurring 30-second timer, so that the pace of data collection continues quickly. Be mindful, though, not to set the timer to too short a time, such that data collection becomes messy."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a28650b5-3593-4c66-bfb1-f7c3e0d7bd5a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00cc85ed-1ebe-46df-ad66-c6194dc3d0d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "63739f9a-c8cd-4fc5-8e27-8e8cd570f2eb",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** If there’s additional time for data collection before the end of class, consider having students collect more data, as by passing their Project Planning Guides between groups."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "63739f9a-c8cd-4fc5-8e27-8e8cd570f2eb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00cc85ed-1ebe-46df-ad66-c6194dc3d0d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c1adb65e-8242-48ae-ad06-f50c0862d39a",
      "position": 23,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Tell students to complete the Day 1 Reflection section in their Project Planning Guide. Prompt students to scan the data they’ve collected, but not to focus on doing any analysis just yet.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c1adb65e-8242-48ae-ad06-f50c0862d39a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "00cc85ed-1ebe-46df-ad66-c6194dc3d0d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "df0ed6f9-927c-4935-9c98-8619b38d40ae",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What is the formatting error in this CSV file?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Review the format of a CSV file. Strong responses will point out that a CSV file uses commas to separate individual values, not spaces. To correctly format the CSV file, we should add commas between the values on each row. Use this discussion as an opportunity to remind students about the format of a CSV file. They will use this knowledge to prepare their data for visualization and analysis.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce what students are doing today.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "df0ed6f9-927c-4935-9c98-8619b38d40ae",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1733f300-f07b-42fc-99fb-32ed4b12d0ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0df5bcf4-7317-4470-8b31-8ca4cfddba5a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Guide students in preparing the data they’ve collected for visualization, as by writing it to, and reading it from, a CSV file. Prompt students to begin visualizing their data, using documentation given to them.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Visualizing Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0df5bcf4-7317-4470-8b31-8ca4cfddba5a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1733f300-f07b-42fc-99fb-32ed4b12d0ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6558f747-8c73-4bd4-8b2e-fc4d4c910059",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Data Research Project"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6558f747-8c73-4bd4-8b2e-fc4d4c910059",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1733f300-f07b-42fc-99fb-32ed4b12d0ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f6748677-ff30-4ecd-9e2a-2f57372f0592",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 12, Levels 1–3.\n- **Level 1:** Students write data from their Project Planning Guide to a CSV file. If students were absent on Day 1, they have a choice of two other datasets.\n- **Level 2:** Students use a helper function to read the data from their CSV file programmatically.\n- **Level 3:** Students read simplified `matplotlib` documentation in their Project Planning Guide to visualize their data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students prepare and visualize their data, look for…\n- **Level 1:** Students who have forgotten the format of a CSV file, or who have made typos while creating their CSV file. Point students to the example CSV file provided in their Project Planning Guide.\n- **Level 2:** Students who encounter errors while using the helper function to read their data from their CSV file. Errors at this stage will occur if a student has not properly formatted their CSV file. Look at their CSV file to ensure it is correctly formatted.\n- **Level 3:** Students who need help getting started. Point them to the \"Getting Started\" section in the `matplotlib` documentation in their Project Planning Guide.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Consider also checking that each student has sufficient data from Day 1. If a student was absent, or does not have usable data, encourage them to choose a predefined dataset in Level 1."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f6748677-ff30-4ecd-9e2a-2f57372f0592",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1733f300-f07b-42fc-99fb-32ed4b12d0ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "227e4010-73f8-487f-a905-0abae991d828",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Tell students to complete the Day 2 Reflection section in their Project Planning Guide.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "227e4010-73f8-487f-a905-0abae991d828",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1733f300-f07b-42fc-99fb-32ed4b12d0ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c22b1c19-8ae8-4018-a146-cf330840b26a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What are the principles of a good visualization? How do these principles show up in your visualization?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to recall the three principles of a good visualization from Unit 3, Lesson 8: clarity, accessibility, and honesty. Strong responses will recall one of these principles and describe a specific way they appear in a student’s visualization: for example, a student might be working to make their visualization honest by choosing appropriate values for the axis scales. If students don’t remember all three principles, remind them about the ones they haven’t mentioned yet.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce what students are doing today.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c22b1c19-8ae8-4018-a146-cf330840b26a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "782ea6a5-2a3a-439d-93a1-50c97282cab4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "008c3266-7c6c-4388-9b7e-6db2527fd42f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Provide time for students to finalize their visualizations and prepare for the gallery walk.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Gallery Walk Preparation"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "008c3266-7c6c-4388-9b7e-6db2527fd42f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "782ea6a5-2a3a-439d-93a1-50c97282cab4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "40d8b681-0404-4c96-ab87-9de3d5181848",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Data Research Project"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "40d8b681-0404-4c96-ab87-9de3d5181848",
        "lesson_activity.key": "782ea6a5-2a3a-439d-93a1-50c97282cab4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e88eb79e-4edc-4f34-96bd-f20362ce6253",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 12, Levels 4–5.\n- **Level 4:** Students make final adjustments to their visualization. They write a summary of their visualization other students will read during the upcoming gallery walk.\n- **Level 5:** Students complete a Check Your Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students add the final touches to their visualization, look for students who want to make specific adjustments but aren’t sure how. Prompt them to read the appropriate documentation, or guide them through the process yourself."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e88eb79e-4edc-4f34-96bd-f20362ce6253",
        "lesson_activity.key": "782ea6a5-2a3a-439d-93a1-50c97282cab4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "50dfacdf-2c12-4302-a7f6-23c8e0c872eb",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Invite students to share their visualizations and to engage with other classmates’ visualizations.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to display their work on Level 4: both their visualization and their visualization summary. Wait until all students have prepared their computers to begin.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct students to circle the room, visiting visualizations of interest to them. As they do, prompt them to fill out the gallery walk reflection questions in their Project Planning Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What did you learn? Which visualizations did you find the most interesting?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to reflect on their experience in the gallery walk. Strong responses will mention a specific visualization they learned something from. Use this time to celebrate what students have created—not just the students who received a shout out in the discussion, but every student who’s now applied what they’ve learned throughout Unit 3.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Gallery Walk"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "50dfacdf-2c12-4302-a7f6-23c8e0c872eb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "782ea6a5-2a3a-439d-93a1-50c97282cab4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2a7f6953-83ef-4071-8697-a91994e0a2e2",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2a7f6953-83ef-4071-8697-a91994e0a2e2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "782ea6a5-2a3a-439d-93a1-50c97282cab4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "457e1369-adbd-408a-9791-fdefb0156a1d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Unit Assessment"
      },
